[{:db/id 17592186389804 :question/problem "Let $I=[0,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=[0,i]\\times \\{i\\}.$ Which of the following represents ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n{⋃}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one}\\,\\,k\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=[0,0.5]\\times \\{0.5\\}=\\{(k,0.5)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,0.5]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a horizontal line segment from the point $(0,0.5)$ to the point $(0.5,0.5)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/75/7587daeaea45c5ef3449ecb676133c60bbedb7819308783bac835f3ae9c6883c.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[0,i]\\times \\{i\\}=\\{(k,i)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,i]\\}\n$$\n\nis the horizontal line segment from the point $(0,i)$ to the point $(i,i).$ \n\n![](images/b9/b926204a3d58b25889f1c79dd0f65c1ea990c67bc88e869004396a77970e8dd4.png)\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following triangle that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}:$\n\n![](images/4a/4a81de1a698eedf6a636d66b68a7c62efa69d1d026a961ae97ab5ecc2ef9828d.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "activity-4ec125ac-e7e6-5672-8f33-3a74502489fe" :activity/id #uuid "4ec125ac-e7e6-5672-8f33-3a74502489fe" :activity/title "Visualizing Cartesian Products" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048206 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-d95f77bb-e077-5521-bcdf-9c73957e19dd" "step-273024b7-a3c5-551a-be72-b7286c27a144" "step-7e3bf0ab-2167-59b1-a2c8-8f2c346337df" "step-b87742e1-5ccb-50d0-bb27-5711cd8741bd" "step-631e5e2d-6b28-58a2-bd13-a4dacbfc7d52"] :activity/first-step "step-d95f77bb-e077-5521-bcdf-9c73957e19dd" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-d95f77bb-e077-5521-bcdf-9c73957e19dd" :step/id #uuid "d95f77bb-e077-5521-bcdf-9c73957e19dd" :step/content 17592186389804 :step/next "step-273024b7-a3c5-551a-be72-b7286c27a144" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-273024b7-a3c5-551a-be72-b7286c27a144" :step/id #uuid "273024b7-a3c5-551a-be72-b7286c27a144" :step/content 17592186389895 :step/next "step-7e3bf0ab-2167-59b1-a2c8-8f2c346337df" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-7e3bf0ab-2167-59b1-a2c8-8f2c346337df" :step/id #uuid "7e3bf0ab-2167-59b1-a2c8-8f2c346337df" :step/content 17592186287749 :step/next "step-b87742e1-5ccb-50d0-bb27-5711cd8741bd" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b87742e1-5ccb-50d0-bb27-5711cd8741bd" :step/id #uuid "b87742e1-5ccb-50d0-bb27-5711cd8741bd" :step/content 17592186287752 :step/next "step-631e5e2d-6b28-58a2-bd13-a4dacbfc7d52" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-631e5e2d-6b28-58a2-bd13-a4dacbfc7d52" :step/id #uuid "631e5e2d-6b28-58a2-bd13-a4dacbfc7d52" :step/content 17592186287875 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162097 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-39aef628-7059-5436-8f38-6e05c93c2909" "question-6b27cf61-4032-5d19-a7e8-2cfee6fa397a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162099 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-236e6a34-eb12-52ba-848b-aa1ea35c9c6a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-39aef628-7059-5436-8f38-6e05c93c2909" :question/id #uuid "39aef628-7059-5436-8f38-6e05c93c2909" :question/math-academy-id "q-139506" :question/problem "A $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are $13$ and $8,$ find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto the ellipse $\\{Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ is $13,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax{‖}^{2}\\}=13\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax‖\\}=\\sqrt{13}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $\\sqrt{13}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2ef5407b-1455-54e1-b51b-9e80211017fa"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2ef5407b-1455-54e1-b51b-9e80211017fa" :answer-field/id #uuid "2ef5407b-1455-54e1-b51b-9e80211017fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9edcd302-0ef2-5f93-959d-3aceccbb61b4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a0b46f98-5ac8-5b4e-bb94-ccb2f5d2c140" "answer-fbbf704b-a180-57cb-8ffd-3202258def89" "answer-8e986c86-71b7-5a51-a24e-17131a654ca7" "answer-102ee921-b567-5226-bf65-b160183a7595" "answer-9edcd302-0ef2-5f93-959d-3aceccbb61b4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a0b46f98-5ac8-5b4e-bb94-ccb2f5d2c140" :answer/id #uuid "a0b46f98-5ac8-5b4e-bb94-ccb2f5d2c140" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "13" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fbbf704b-a180-57cb-8ffd-3202258def89" :answer/id #uuid "fbbf704b-a180-57cb-8ffd-3202258def89" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8e986c86-71b7-5a51-a24e-17131a654ca7" :answer/id #uuid "8e986c86-71b7-5a51-a24e-17131a654ca7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-102ee921-b567-5226-bf65-b160183a7595" :answer/id #uuid "102ee921-b567-5226-bf65-b160183a7595" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9edcd302-0ef2-5f93-959d-3aceccbb61b4" :answer/id #uuid "9edcd302-0ef2-5f93-959d-3aceccbb61b4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6b27cf61-4032-5d19-a7e8-2cfee6fa397a" :question/id #uuid "6b27cf61-4032-5d19-a7e8-2cfee6fa397a" :question/math-academy-id "q-139531" :question/problem "A $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are $12$ and $7,$ find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto the ellipse $\\{Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ is $12,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax{‖}^{2}\\}=12\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax‖\\}=\\sqrt{12}=2\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $2\\sqrt{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-756b7007-2354-5f57-a27e-e64df3e652a9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-756b7007-2354-5f57-a27e-e64df3e652a9" :answer-field/id #uuid "756b7007-2354-5f57-a27e-e64df3e652a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d56c67fa-abc8-5c4a-9511-24fe90b45abb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0d697f1a-91d4-5ea3-899b-c3408ee2ab1c" "answer-d56c67fa-abc8-5c4a-9511-24fe90b45abb" "answer-c87c11da-4ee7-5c82-9fac-22443d8cbc9d" "answer-54d38533-d806-50e5-a7f3-9d141a602fc7" "answer-e21caf68-2b09-5596-859d-4d5d49e08b4f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0d697f1a-91d4-5ea3-899b-c3408ee2ab1c" :answer/id #uuid "0d697f1a-91d4-5ea3-899b-c3408ee2ab1c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d56c67fa-abc8-5c4a-9511-24fe90b45abb" :answer/id #uuid "d56c67fa-abc8-5c4a-9511-24fe90b45abb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c87c11da-4ee7-5c82-9fac-22443d8cbc9d" :answer/id #uuid "c87c11da-4ee7-5c82-9fac-22443d8cbc9d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-54d38533-d806-50e5-a7f3-9d141a602fc7" :answer/id #uuid "54d38533-d806-50e5-a7f3-9d141a602fc7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e21caf68-2b09-5596-859d-4d5d49e08b4f" :answer/id #uuid "e21caf68-2b09-5596-859d-4d5d49e08b4f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186269236 :question/problem "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are $15,$ $10,$ and $0,$ find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse $\\{Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ is $15,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax{‖}^{2}\\}=15\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax‖\\}=\\sqrt{15}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $\\sqrt{15}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162364 :question/problem "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are $7,$ $3,$ and $0,$ find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse $\\{Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ is $7,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax{‖}^{2}\\}=7\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax‖\\}=\\sqrt{7}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $\\sqrt{7}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186159386 :answer-field/correct 17592186159390 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186269230 :question/problem "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are $12,$ $9,$ and $0,$ find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse $\\{Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ is $12,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax{‖}^{2}\\}=12\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax‖\\}=\\sqrt{12}=2\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $2\\sqrt{3}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-236e6a34-eb12-52ba-848b-aa1ea35c9c6a" :question/id #uuid "236e6a34-eb12-52ba-848b-aa1ea35c9c6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-139533" :question/problem "A $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=6$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=2\\sqrt{3},$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto the ellipse $\\{Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=6,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={6}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax{‖}^{2}\\}={6}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax‖\\}=6.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=6.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-97f16011-48a5-5603-ae76-0cd52cee0497"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-97f16011-48a5-5603-ae76-0cd52cee0497" :answer-field/id #uuid "97f16011-48a5-5603-ae76-0cd52cee0497" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a13015bd-c5c8-578c-9c60-786d446af287" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9407fc80-2f45-5795-8b6d-8d17d520c654" "answer-2c81dabb-dfd7-5bfa-a4eb-6f813166334c" "answer-2b3b4afb-fa95-55b9-bb95-7bd1163684f6" "answer-a13015bd-c5c8-578c-9c60-786d446af287" "answer-ee940030-c583-5d51-8be9-b9494cf6a665"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9407fc80-2f45-5795-8b6d-8d17d520c654" :answer/id #uuid "9407fc80-2f45-5795-8b6d-8d17d520c654" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2c81dabb-dfd7-5bfa-a4eb-6f813166334c" :answer/id #uuid "2c81dabb-dfd7-5bfa-a4eb-6f813166334c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2b3b4afb-fa95-55b9-bb95-7bd1163684f6" :answer/id #uuid "2b3b4afb-fa95-55b9-bb95-7bd1163684f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a13015bd-c5c8-578c-9c60-786d446af287" :answer/id #uuid "a13015bd-c5c8-578c-9c60-786d446af287" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ee940030-c583-5d51-8be9-b9494cf6a665" :answer/id #uuid "ee940030-c583-5d51-8be9-b9494cf6a665" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186269241 :question/problem "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=5,$ ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{2},$ and ${\\sigma }_{3}=0,$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse $\\{Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=5,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={5}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax{‖}^{2}\\}={5}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax‖\\}=5.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=5.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162361 :question/problem "A $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=10$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=2\\sqrt{5},$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto the ellipse $\\{Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=10,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={10}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax{‖}^{2}\\}={10}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax‖\\}=10.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=10.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186269242 :question/problem "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=6,$ ${\\sigma }_{2}=4,$ and ${\\sigma }_{3}=0,$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse $\\{Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=6,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={6}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax{‖}^{2}\\}={6}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax‖\\}=6.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=6.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186383424 :question/problem "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=7,$ ${\\sigma }_{2}=2\\sqrt{3},$ and ${\\sigma }_{3}=0,$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse $\\{Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=7,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={7}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax{‖}^{2}\\}={7}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax‖\\}=7.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=7.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186269245 :question/problem "![](images/6b/6be1f51600ae7b42ad0c4c8f67eb8f3ba3570606ec4e3e5e75c65df397aab060.png)\n\nA $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ what are the nonzero singular values of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}\\}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=7={\\sigma }_{1},$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=5={\\sigma }_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=7$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=5.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186369748 :question/problem "![](images/f9/f9b7a55ee1b8d3327ae9da740feaafde459daaee4e42a27cf26fd0ac8e63f20b.png)\n\nA $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ which of the following is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $36$ of ${A}^{T}A?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}\\}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=6={\\sigma }_{1},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{1}={6}^{2}=36,$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=2={\\sigma }_{2},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{2}={2}^{2}=4.$\n\nTherefore, an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=36$ of ${A}^{T}A$ is ${v}_{1}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186269246 :question/problem "![](images/2d/2d1cefc0c789518d21279c5c49a18f80220a2aad80973f0e84fda0ddaf25493b.png)\n\nA $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ which of the following is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $100$ of ${A}^{T}A?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}\\}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=10={\\sigma }_{1},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{1}={10}^{2}=100,$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=8={\\sigma }_{2},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{2}={8}^{2}=64.$\n\nTherefore, an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=100$ of ${A}^{T}A$ is ${v}_{1}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186400763 :question/problem "![](images/6d/6df3639cb222756221803446ebc735cf1e7cbcf4a922ed7fce835c0c44fb9aa1.png)\n\nA $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ what are the nonzero singular values of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}\\}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=5={\\sigma }_{1},$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=3={\\sigma }_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=5$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=3.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186400770 :question/problem "![](images/57/57368434b3025dc777c5be725da43e365dfd8ef3c47049570c0ecb13f2baf035.png)\n\nA $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ what are the nonzero singular values of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}\\}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=9={\\sigma }_{1},$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=4={\\sigma }_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=9$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186106135 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$, given the points $A(1,2,-1)$, $B(2,3,1)$, $C(4,1,2)$, and $D(1,4,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(2i+3j+k)-(i+2j-k) \\\\  & =i+j+2k \\\\  & =⟨1,1,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(4i+j+2k)-(i+2j-k) \\\\  & =3i-j+3k \\\\  & =⟨3,-1,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(i+4j+2k)-(i+2j-k) \\\\  & =2j+3k \\\\  & =⟨0,2,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|+2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot 3-2\\cdot 3)-(3\\cdot 3-0\\cdot 3)+2(3\\cdot 2-0\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =-9-9+12 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelpiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-6|=6.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186102686 :answer-field/correct 17592186102688 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105908 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2b5a6dbe-bec1-5af9-82e5-d252aa38eb1f" "question-b407ea24-4cac-5cb8-bbfb-b1a7b706dc73"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-2b5a6dbe-bec1-5af9-82e5-d252aa38eb1f" :question/id #uuid "2b5a6dbe-bec1-5af9-82e5-d252aa38eb1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-73733" :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $a=⟨-1,1,1⟩$, $b=⟨2,1,0⟩$, and $c=⟨k,-2,-3⟩$ are coplanar." :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ k & -2 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}|-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ k & -3\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ k & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(1\\cdot (-3)-(-2)\\cdot 0)-(2\\cdot (-3)-k\\cdot 0)+(2\\cdot (-2)-k\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =3+6-4-k \\\\  & =5-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =0 \\\\ 5-k & =0 \\\\ k & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0b436b69-27e7-557e-9669-3a80b22007ad"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0b436b69-27e7-557e-9669-3a80b22007ad" :answer-field/id #uuid "0b436b69-27e7-557e-9669-3a80b22007ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2cc5ee62-4f20-5c54-9e5a-bbb471927bb4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ae7cc567-6679-5581-b389-e82348eb1ae3" "answer-9fcfd0be-c665-50f6-b594-87f4f9162f0b" "answer-2cc5ee62-4f20-5c54-9e5a-bbb471927bb4" "answer-3135cadf-898b-5d47-89f0-df3060a008eb" "answer-389cf47a-7d3c-5e4e-b0ed-207e5d8a0087"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae7cc567-6679-5581-b389-e82348eb1ae3" :answer/id #uuid "ae7cc567-6679-5581-b389-e82348eb1ae3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "13" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9fcfd0be-c665-50f6-b594-87f4f9162f0b" :answer/id #uuid "9fcfd0be-c665-50f6-b594-87f4f9162f0b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2cc5ee62-4f20-5c54-9e5a-bbb471927bb4" :answer/id #uuid "2cc5ee62-4f20-5c54-9e5a-bbb471927bb4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3135cadf-898b-5d47-89f0-df3060a008eb" :answer/id #uuid "3135cadf-898b-5d47-89f0-df3060a008eb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-389cf47a-7d3c-5e4e-b0ed-207e5d8a0087" :answer/id #uuid "389cf47a-7d3c-5e4e-b0ed-207e5d8a0087" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b407ea24-4cac-5cb8-bbfb-b1a7b706dc73" :question/id #uuid "b407ea24-4cac-5cb8-bbfb-b1a7b706dc73" :question/math-academy-id "q-280770" :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $x=⟨1,k,2⟩$, $y=⟨5,-2,3⟩$, and $z=⟨-4,k,1⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (y\\times z) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & k & 2 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -4 & k & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ k & 1\\end{aligned}|-k\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}|+2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -4 & k\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(-2\\cdot 1-3\\cdot k)-k(5\\cdot 1-3\\cdot -4)+2(5k-(-2)(-4)) \\\\  & =-2-3k-k(5+12)+2(5k-8) \\\\  & =-2-3k-17k+10k-16 \\\\  & =-10k-18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (y\\times z) & =0 \\\\ -10k-18 & =0 \\\\ k & =-\\frac{9}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-\\frac{9}{5}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a9d8c1a0-222c-5144-9f47-a1a87a86c092"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a9d8c1a0-222c-5144-9f47-a1a87a86c092" :answer-field/id #uuid "a9d8c1a0-222c-5144-9f47-a1a87a86c092" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-a627e562-14bd-588f-a5f4-1d17e6cb6828" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a627e562-14bd-588f-a5f4-1d17e6cb6828"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a627e562-14bd-588f-a5f4-1d17e6cb6828" :answer/id #uuid "a627e562-14bd-588f-a5f4-1d17e6cb6828" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-3f085bc3-d4e1-5959-bd6a-cbbb58662438" :activity/id #uuid "3f085bc3-d4e1-5959-bd6a-cbbb58662438" :activity/title "Volumes of Parallelepipeds" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049335 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-9044800c-37a9-5a39-94d5-148ee640cb1e" "step-9453de60-3efa-5f31-981a-06a36470b7cb" "step-e6521478-5710-54f7-9cae-ff12b96891b0" "step-7627e8da-c0a2-598b-b7d9-ae6f8ff09f19" "step-2818fbd2-4e8e-5ddd-8195-a9f944008965"] :activity/first-step "step-9044800c-37a9-5a39-94d5-148ee640cb1e" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-9044800c-37a9-5a39-94d5-148ee640cb1e" :step/id #uuid "9044800c-37a9-5a39-94d5-148ee640cb1e" :step/content 17592186106135 :step/next "step-9453de60-3efa-5f31-981a-06a36470b7cb" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-9453de60-3efa-5f31-981a-06a36470b7cb" :step/id #uuid "9453de60-3efa-5f31-981a-06a36470b7cb" :step/content "question-2b5a6dbe-bec1-5af9-82e5-d252aa38eb1f" :step/next "step-e6521478-5710-54f7-9cae-ff12b96891b0" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e6521478-5710-54f7-9cae-ff12b96891b0" :step/id #uuid "e6521478-5710-54f7-9cae-ff12b96891b0" :step/content 17592186262907 :step/next "step-7627e8da-c0a2-598b-b7d9-ae6f8ff09f19" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-7627e8da-c0a2-598b-b7d9-ae6f8ff09f19" :step/id #uuid "7627e8da-c0a2-598b-b7d9-ae6f8ff09f19" :step/content "question-b407ea24-4cac-5cb8-bbfb-b1a7b706dc73" :step/next "step-2818fbd2-4e8e-5ddd-8195-a9f944008965" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-2818fbd2-4e8e-5ddd-8195-a9f944008965" :step/id #uuid "2818fbd2-4e8e-5ddd-8195-a9f944008965" :step/content 17592186295176 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186109201 :answer-field/choices 17592186109202] [:db/retract 17592186109201 :answer-field/choices 17592186109203] [:db/retract 17592186109201 :answer-field/choices 17592186109204] [:db/retract 17592186109201 :answer-field/choices 17592186109205] [:db/retract 17592186109201 :answer-field/choices 17592186109206] {:db/id 17592186110760 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0bd5f670-a4a8-511a-9fe4-89d9e2250371" "question-679aea70-862a-57d5-bcef-459256cb248c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110390 :question/problem "![](images/80/800f11f555f0c2be0e89aa26602cfe4a35a62f4fe2309dfe2a188f47138fb98b.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above. Which of the following statements is true?\n\nI. $\\angle A$ is obtuse\n\nII. $\\angle B$ is a right angle\n\nIII. $\\angle A$ and $\\angle B$ are vertical angles" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the diagram, $\\angle A$ and the angle with measure $130^\\circ$ are vertical angles. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm\\angle A = 130^\\circ >    90^\\circ.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\angle A$ is an obtuse angle.\n\n- Statement II is false. From the diagram, $\\angle B$ and the angle with measure $50^\\circ$ are vertical angles. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm\\angle B = 50^\\circ \\neq    90^\\circ.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\angle B$ is not a right angle.\n\n- Statement III is false. Angles $\\angle A$ and $\\angle B$  are not vertical since they have a common side.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110761 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b814d590-16e2-55cf-89ba-ea5367397c7b" "question-be894050-f250-5773-8720-f2e3adc2c8cc"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110391 :question/worked-solution "Since $\\angle S$ is a straight angle, we have\n\n$$\nm\\angle S=180^\\circ,\n$$\n\nand since $\\angle T$ is a right angle, we have \n\n$$\nm\\angle T=90^{\\circ}.\n$$\n\nSo, their sum is \n\n$$\nm\\angle S + m\\angle T=180^{\\circ} + 90^{\\circ}=270^{\\circ}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sum of $\\angle S$ and $\\angle T$ is a reflex angle." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110670 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b151647b-d6b1-5228-a002-474a2ce605db" "question-b6c23b91-4fda-5c9a-8297-f36bd4ddd1a4"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110291 :question/problem "![](images/44/44aa56708e43f0518d7759e96b0770834f999998280c52910b2ca07cd607c1ab.png)\n\nGiven that $\\angle DOA$ is a straight angle, what is the value of $x?$" :question/worked-solution "Note that $\\angle AOD$ is a straight angle and $\\angle BOC$ is a right angle. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠AOD={180}^{∘},\\,m∠BOC={90}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠AOB+m∠BOC+m∠COD & =m∠AOD \\\\ x+{90}^{∘}+(2x-{30}^{∘}) & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 3x+{60}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 3x & ={120}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={40}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110759 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7352f5cb-3930-54b5-bef8-2eba5dc04e5f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110387 :question/problem "In the figure shown below, determine the measure of $\\angle EOA.$\n\n![](images/80/80f47745a289bf021a4566a58d1961c93bb216e2df900b8effae8f2dda3f1606.png)" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, $\\angle COD$ is a right angle, so\n\n$$\nm\\angle COD = 90^\\circ.\n$$\n\nAlso, since $\\angle COD$ and $\\angle EOB$ are vertical angles, they have the same measure:\n\n$$\nm\\angle EOB=m\\angle COD=90^\\circ\n$$\n\nNow, since we know that $m\\angle AOB=40^{\\circ}$ and $m\\angle EOB = 90^\\circ,$ we can use the angle addition postulate to solve for $m\\angle EOA\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠EOA+m∠AOB & =m∠EOB \\\\ m∠EOA+{40}^{∘} & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ m∠EOA & ={50}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-0bd5f670-a4a8-511a-9fe4-89d9e2250371" :question/id #uuid "0bd5f670-a4a8-511a-9fe4-89d9e2250371" :question/math-academy-id "q-52736" :question/problem "![](images/ae/aec20235a2968f30f1ac3861d429a4102464a701fff0a90c13dae97334735273.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above. Which of the following statements is true?\n\nI. $∠A$ is null\n\nII. $∠B$ is acute\n\nIII. $∠C$ is obtuse" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. From the diagram, it follows that $m∠A={180}^{∘}>{0}^{∘}.$\n\n- Statement II is true, and statement III is false. Indeed, from the graph we deduce  that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠B+{100}^{∘}+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠B+m∠C & ={80}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the measure of each of the angles $∠B$ and $∠C$ is less than ${80}^{∘},$ so these angles are acute angles.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4d9fbb57-14ec-5a87-a6ae-af2fa3a7feb6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4d9fbb57-14ec-5a87-a6ae-af2fa3a7feb6" :answer-field/id #uuid "4d9fbb57-14ec-5a87-a6ae-af2fa3a7feb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3fe50095-7958-5765-aef7-914d268ee8d0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8a84c6f1-4efc-5f12-acaa-6660ac2f2a91" "answer-32286793-c28f-5862-a676-dfdcef594030" "answer-80cf6eca-cd51-5611-a089-8312085b8c17" "answer-3fe50095-7958-5765-aef7-914d268ee8d0" "answer-1f35d0a0-72de-5c1b-8fef-a7aa761262ba"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8a84c6f1-4efc-5f12-acaa-6660ac2f2a91" :answer/id #uuid "8a84c6f1-4efc-5f12-acaa-6660ac2f2a91" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-32286793-c28f-5862-a676-dfdcef594030" :answer/id #uuid "32286793-c28f-5862-a676-dfdcef594030" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-80cf6eca-cd51-5611-a089-8312085b8c17" :answer/id #uuid "80cf6eca-cd51-5611-a089-8312085b8c17" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3fe50095-7958-5765-aef7-914d268ee8d0" :answer/id #uuid "3fe50095-7958-5765-aef7-914d268ee8d0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1f35d0a0-72de-5c1b-8fef-a7aa761262ba" :answer/id #uuid "1f35d0a0-72de-5c1b-8fef-a7aa761262ba" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-679aea70-862a-57d5-bcef-459256cb248c" :question/id #uuid "679aea70-862a-57d5-bcef-459256cb248c" :question/math-academy-id "q-76777" :question/problem "![](images/64/64fcf64e043698366083e24b6041e2fb406b725671ab71c0c871c7e708517685.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above. Which of the following statements is true?\n\nI. $∠A$ is a null angle\n\nII. $∠C$ is a full angle\n\nIII. $∠D$ is a right angle" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. From the diagram, $m∠A>{0}^{∘}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. From the diagram, $m∠C<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. From the diagram, $m∠D={90}^{∘}.$ Therefore, $∠D$ is a right angle.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ec0276f1-96e6-52f4-9303-7a2cc6a4348f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ec0276f1-96e6-52f4-9303-7a2cc6a4348f" :answer-field/id #uuid "ec0276f1-96e6-52f4-9303-7a2cc6a4348f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-cfa1faed-e1c5-57b8-a670-8810e5610649" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9038dbdd-8eae-5460-b48d-1f1d998e1a9f" "answer-dcc42be0-d433-5c09-92d2-0ad1be46916f" "answer-3bdfb115-f1cf-595c-b0a6-1d8a5caa3a92" "answer-b143655e-9c66-5750-b57b-9f00bc54e9ed" "answer-cfa1faed-e1c5-57b8-a670-8810e5610649"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9038dbdd-8eae-5460-b48d-1f1d998e1a9f" :answer/id #uuid "9038dbdd-8eae-5460-b48d-1f1d998e1a9f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dcc42be0-d433-5c09-92d2-0ad1be46916f" :answer/id #uuid "dcc42be0-d433-5c09-92d2-0ad1be46916f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3bdfb115-f1cf-595c-b0a6-1d8a5caa3a92" :answer/id #uuid "3bdfb115-f1cf-595c-b0a6-1d8a5caa3a92" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b143655e-9c66-5750-b57b-9f00bc54e9ed" :answer/id #uuid "b143655e-9c66-5750-b57b-9f00bc54e9ed" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cfa1faed-e1c5-57b8-a670-8810e5610649" :answer/id #uuid "cfa1faed-e1c5-57b8-a670-8810e5610649" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b814d590-16e2-55cf-89ba-ea5367397c7b" :question/id #uuid "b814d590-16e2-55cf-89ba-ea5367397c7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-52745" :question/problem "Let $∠S$ and $∠T$ be two adjacent angles. Given that $∠S$ is a straight angle and $∠T$ is obtuse, what type of angle is their sum?" :question/worked-solution "Since $∠S$ is a straight angle, we have \n\n$$\nm∠S={180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\n Since $∠T$ is obtuse, we have\n\n$$\n{90}^{∘}<m∠T<{180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of these two angles satisfies by the following inequalies:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}+{90}^{∘} & <m∠S+m∠T<{180}^{∘}+{180}^{∘} \\\\ {270}^{∘} & <m∠S+m∠T<{360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sum of $∠S$ and $∠T$ is a reflex angle." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e85777c4-f5a5-5556-baa2-3cf1a0f7f89c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e85777c4-f5a5-5556-baa2-3cf1a0f7f89c" :answer-field/id #uuid "e85777c4-f5a5-5556-baa2-3cf1a0f7f89c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b3842f72-fd00-53f0-8fc3-139197c1f664" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0c479774-aa4a-5084-a50f-b935229aed16" "answer-eeef453f-c7fd-5acd-9659-9446f38f849b" "answer-a973a754-84a1-5793-8718-0ba38046f99a" "answer-b3842f72-fd00-53f0-8fc3-139197c1f664" "answer-bfb9aa9e-8ced-5049-8416-7993780d0987"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0c479774-aa4a-5084-a50f-b935229aed16" :answer/id #uuid "0c479774-aa4a-5084-a50f-b935229aed16" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Acute" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eeef453f-c7fd-5acd-9659-9446f38f849b" :answer/id #uuid "eeef453f-c7fd-5acd-9659-9446f38f849b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Full" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a973a754-84a1-5793-8718-0ba38046f99a" :answer/id #uuid "a973a754-84a1-5793-8718-0ba38046f99a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Obtuse" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b3842f72-fd00-53f0-8fc3-139197c1f664" :answer/id #uuid "b3842f72-fd00-53f0-8fc3-139197c1f664" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Reflex" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bfb9aa9e-8ced-5049-8416-7993780d0987" :answer/id #uuid "bfb9aa9e-8ced-5049-8416-7993780d0987" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Straight" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-be894050-f250-5773-8720-f2e3adc2c8cc" :question/id #uuid "be894050-f250-5773-8720-f2e3adc2c8cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-118297" :question/problem "Let $∠N$ and $∠S$ be two adjacent angles. Given that $∠N$ is a null angle and $∠S$ is straight, what type of angle is their sum?" :question/worked-solution "Since $∠N$ is a null and a right angle, we have\n\n$$\nm∠N={0}^{∘}\n$$\n\nand since $∠S$ is a straight angle, we have\n\n$$\nm∠S={180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, their sum is \n\n$$\nm∠N+m∠S={0}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}={180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sum of $∠N$ and $∠S$ is a straight angle." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e8676a48-f37e-575d-b431-78b2e10570aa"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e8676a48-f37e-575d-b431-78b2e10570aa" :answer-field/id #uuid "e8676a48-f37e-575d-b431-78b2e10570aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7cf6b57f-13bc-5f45-a7b4-af650da0ae8a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-29ab0cf8-3f34-59c0-8e21-66edfaf04089" "answer-3e34a591-6a79-58e5-8bf2-b28cbeb7a5ab" "answer-5a2ca949-167e-5ea3-8f87-0bd5403b151a" "answer-78ed045b-f25b-5f3b-9f9b-72d9de4e2108" "answer-7cf6b57f-13bc-5f45-a7b4-af650da0ae8a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-29ab0cf8-3f34-59c0-8e21-66edfaf04089" :answer/id #uuid "29ab0cf8-3f34-59c0-8e21-66edfaf04089" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Full" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3e34a591-6a79-58e5-8bf2-b28cbeb7a5ab" :answer/id #uuid "3e34a591-6a79-58e5-8bf2-b28cbeb7a5ab" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Obtuse" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5a2ca949-167e-5ea3-8f87-0bd5403b151a" :answer/id #uuid "5a2ca949-167e-5ea3-8f87-0bd5403b151a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Reflex" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-78ed045b-f25b-5f3b-9f9b-72d9de4e2108" :answer/id #uuid "78ed045b-f25b-5f3b-9f9b-72d9de4e2108" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Right" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7cf6b57f-13bc-5f45-a7b4-af650da0ae8a" :answer/id #uuid "7cf6b57f-13bc-5f45-a7b4-af650da0ae8a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Straight" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b151647b-d6b1-5228-a002-474a2ce605db" :question/id #uuid "b151647b-d6b1-5228-a002-474a2ce605db" :question/math-academy-id "q-52647" :question/problem "![](images/a5/a5752cefaad576ffeb18408db80dac554a525a0e4137b46377fee225436da433.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, $∠AOB≅∠COD$ and $∠BOC≅∠DOE.$ Given that $m∠AOB=2m∠BOC$ and that $∠AOE$ is a straight angle, $m∠BOC=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $∠AOB≅∠COD$ and $∠DOE≅∠BOC,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠AOB & =m∠COD \\\\ m∠BOC & =m∠DOE\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, since $∠AOE$ is a straight angle, we have $m∠AOE={180}^{∘}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠AOB+m∠BOC+m∠COD+m∠DOE & =m∠AOE \\\\ m∠AOB+m∠BOC+m∠AOB+m∠BOC & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 2m∠BOC+m∠BOC+2m∠BOC+m∠BOC & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 6m∠BOC & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠BOC & ={30}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7ec9975b-d6f8-5646-b0e0-edaca8358321"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7ec9975b-d6f8-5646-b0e0-edaca8358321" :answer-field/id #uuid "7ec9975b-d6f8-5646-b0e0-edaca8358321" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6d96124d-f237-5464-9fe0-f5821f8ccf68" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c928eb56-fbb8-5dc6-b810-b590302de8de" "answer-02975f11-2d2d-5929-8588-5012e8b84c2b" "answer-6d96124d-f237-5464-9fe0-f5821f8ccf68" "answer-57826d32-8995-54d3-b84a-ec44c68faf1b" "answer-c0bf48eb-6be6-56cd-b586-74274b956554"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c928eb56-fbb8-5dc6-b810-b590302de8de" :answer/id #uuid "c928eb56-fbb8-5dc6-b810-b590302de8de" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{15}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-02975f11-2d2d-5929-8588-5012e8b84c2b" :answer/id #uuid "02975f11-2d2d-5929-8588-5012e8b84c2b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{20}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6d96124d-f237-5464-9fe0-f5821f8ccf68" :answer/id #uuid "6d96124d-f237-5464-9fe0-f5821f8ccf68" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-57826d32-8995-54d3-b84a-ec44c68faf1b" :answer/id #uuid "57826d32-8995-54d3-b84a-ec44c68faf1b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c0bf48eb-6be6-56cd-b586-74274b956554" :answer/id #uuid "c0bf48eb-6be6-56cd-b586-74274b956554" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{60}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b6c23b91-4fda-5c9a-8297-f36bd4ddd1a4" :question/id #uuid "b6c23b91-4fda-5c9a-8297-f36bd4ddd1a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-53173" :question/problem "![](images/d6/d607ba743afcc7d39299278e0d352e2c644b587480834a5c68cf2b46687b7f73.png)\n\nWhat is the value of $t$ given that $∠VOZ$ is a right angle?" :question/worked-solution "Since $∠VOZ$ is a right angle, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠VOZ={90}^{∘},\\,m∠VOW=m∠YOZ={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can form an equation and solve for $t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠VOW+m∠WOX+m∠XOY+m∠YOZ & =m∠VOZ \\\\ {30}^{∘}+t+t+{30}^{∘} & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ 2t & ={30}^{∘} \\\\ t & ={15}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3a96b7f7-43ca-537f-a8b8-336694a0d953"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3a96b7f7-43ca-537f-a8b8-336694a0d953" :answer-field/id #uuid "3a96b7f7-43ca-537f-a8b8-336694a0d953" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-713314d9-62ce-573b-96ab-e10c6a7069d9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-698dc0ce-e75a-5496-830d-7bb5cb3e368e" "answer-713314d9-62ce-573b-96ab-e10c6a7069d9" "answer-96cae35a-e83c-5482-a7dc-0a00c6cae34b" "answer-409f8837-a9cd-5b82-a2d7-a206faa30f96" "answer-8c7d0d7c-0d46-5f63-ac35-435f9bfd536b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-698dc0ce-e75a-5496-830d-7bb5cb3e368e" :answer/id #uuid "698dc0ce-e75a-5496-830d-7bb5cb3e368e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{10}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-713314d9-62ce-573b-96ab-e10c6a7069d9" :answer/id #uuid "713314d9-62ce-573b-96ab-e10c6a7069d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{15}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-96cae35a-e83c-5482-a7dc-0a00c6cae34b" :answer/id #uuid "96cae35a-e83c-5482-a7dc-0a00c6cae34b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{20}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-409f8837-a9cd-5b82-a2d7-a206faa30f96" :answer/id #uuid "409f8837-a9cd-5b82-a2d7-a206faa30f96" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{25}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8c7d0d7c-0d46-5f63-ac35-435f9bfd536b" :answer/id #uuid "8c7d0d7c-0d46-5f63-ac35-435f9bfd536b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186111246 :question/problem "![](images/1f/1fab2b2158183c89fb48259408e3a1c684af657cd34d68d4b79e4773a4251f37.png)\n\nIn the figure shown above, determine the measure of $∠GOH$ given that $m∠BOD={120}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, $∠AOC$ and $∠EOG$ are vertical angles. Since vertical angles are congruent, we have\n\n$$\nm∠AOC=m∠EOG={90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, since $∠AOC$ is a right angle, the angle addition postulate gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠AOB+m∠BOC & =m∠AOC \\\\ {30}^{∘}+m∠BOC & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ m∠BOC & ={60}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, given that $m∠BOD={120}^{∘}$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BOC+m∠COD & =m∠BOD \\\\ {60}^{∘}+m∠COD & ={120}^{∘} \\\\ m∠COD & ={60}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $∠COD$ and $∠GOH$ are vertical angles. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm∠GOH=m∠COD={60}^{∘}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186109201 :answer-field/correct "answer-2086e39b-cf43-5217-9c01-57d002497a13" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cd0b9a45-f419-5238-94d4-94c1a2452f82" "answer-e9e9243e-ffd6-5efc-991d-1dcba24e6b57" "answer-2086e39b-cf43-5217-9c01-57d002497a13" "answer-e57e86ef-8464-506b-b83e-b486bc299195" "answer-f6d1d50a-79d8-5dd9-91c7-92b34f96b05a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd0b9a45-f419-5238-94d4-94c1a2452f82" :answer/id #uuid "cd0b9a45-f419-5238-94d4-94c1a2452f82" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{50}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e9e9243e-ffd6-5efc-991d-1dcba24e6b57" :answer/id #uuid "e9e9243e-ffd6-5efc-991d-1dcba24e6b57" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{55}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2086e39b-cf43-5217-9c01-57d002497a13" :answer/id #uuid "2086e39b-cf43-5217-9c01-57d002497a13" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{60}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e57e86ef-8464-506b-b83e-b486bc299195" :answer/id #uuid "e57e86ef-8464-506b-b83e-b486bc299195" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{65}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f6d1d50a-79d8-5dd9-91c7-92b34f96b05a" :answer/id #uuid "f6d1d50a-79d8-5dd9-91c7-92b34f96b05a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{70}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7352f5cb-3930-54b5-bef8-2eba5dc04e5f" :question/id #uuid "7352f5cb-3930-54b5-bef8-2eba5dc04e5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-171812" :question/problem "![](images/be/be311b9b624fba745b93d7ac3cd67ace3bbe4ea0650b12fa900d3aac50b5bae2.png)\n\nIn the figure shown above, the measure of $∠COF$ is{{field-1}}\n${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "The angle addition postulate gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠EOD & =m∠EOB+m∠BOD \\\\  & ={82}^{∘}+{47}^{∘} \\\\  & ={129}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, $∠AOF$ and $∠EOD$ are vertical angles. Since vertical angles are congruent, we have \n\n$$\nm∠AOF=m∠EOD={129}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, since $∠AOC$ is a right angle, the angle addition postulate gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠AOF & =m∠AOC+m∠COF \\\\ {129}^{∘} & ={90}^{∘}+m∠COF \\\\ m∠COF & ={39}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-40413315-c578-5bd8-a689-73bf0674b2fe"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-40413315-c578-5bd8-a689-73bf0674b2fe" :answer-field/id #uuid "40413315-c578-5bd8-a689-73bf0674b2fe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-bbfb00a3-d7b9-5630-b87d-050558a3b0c5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bbfb00a3-d7b9-5630-b87d-050558a3b0c5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bbfb00a3-d7b9-5630-b87d-050558a3b0c5" :answer/id #uuid "bbfb00a3-d7b9-5630-b87d-050558a3b0c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "39" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186111840 :tutorial/content "When solving problems with angles, it is helpful to remember the following classifications:\n\n- a **null** angle has a measure of $0^\\circ,$\n\n- an **acute** angle has a measure between $0^\\circ$ and $90^\\circ,$\n\n- a **right** angle has a measure of $90^\\circ,$\n\n- an **obtuse** angle has a measure between $90^\\circ$ and $180^\\circ,$\n\n- a **straight** angle has a measure of $180^\\circ,$\n\n- a **reflex** angle has a measure between $180^\\circ$ and $360^\\circ,$ and\n\n- a **full** angle has a measure of $360^\\circ.$\n\nIt is also helpful to remember that when two lines meet at a point, the opposite angles are called **vertical angles**. Vertical angles are congruent, and consequently they have the same measure." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110823 :activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186417871 :question/problem "Let $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $a=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ If $x=y+z,$ where $y\\in \\text{Span}\\{a\\}$ and $z⊥\\text{Span}\\{a\\},$ then $z=$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/d8/d887852fe51f08cb7296437e69b6a9632721cfd155558e6abf9a99b4f59f7ad6.png)\n\nFirst, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{a\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,x & =\\frac{a\\cdot x}{a\\cdot a}a \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 7+(-3)(-6)+1\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}}a \\\\  & =\\frac{6+21+18+1}{4+9+9+1}a \\\\  & =\\frac{46}{23}a \\\\  & =2a \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ z & =x-{\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $y\\in \\text{Span}\\{a\\}$ and $z⊥\\text{Span}\\{a\\},$ as shown in the picture above.\n\nIn conclusion, $z=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186326840 :question/problem "Let $x=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$ Find the distance between $x$ and $\\text{Span}\\{b\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "The distance between the vector $x$ and the vector space $\\text{Span}\\{b\\}$ is given by $‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖$, where $\\overset{^}{x}$ in the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto $\\text{span}\\{b\\}.$\n\nFirst, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{b\\}.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,x & =\\frac{b\\cdot x}{b\\cdot b}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot (-2)+(-1)\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-2)+(-1)(-2)}{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{-2-2-2+2}{1+1+1+1}b \\\\  & =-b \\\\  & =-1[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}+({x}_{4}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{4})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{((-2)-(-1))^{2}+(2-1)^{2}+((-2)-(-1))^{2}+((-2)-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1+1+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134288 :question/problem "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find a basis of the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nFinally, we find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot b}{b\\cdot b}b \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot 1}{{(-1)}^{2}+{1}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{-8}{2}b \\\\  & =-4b \\\\  & =-4[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186394140 :question/problem "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find a basis of the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -5 & -8 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -8 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are $2$ pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. Now, from the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=2{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-3\\cdot (2{x}_{3})=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=6{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nFinally, we find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot b}{b\\cdot b}b \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\cdot 6+5\\cdot 2+0\\cdot 1}{{6}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{82}{41}b \\\\  & =2b \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186417869 :question/problem "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find a basis of the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are $2$ pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. Now, from the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=2{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-3\\cdot (2{x}_{3})+{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=5{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nFinally, we find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot b}{b\\cdot b}b \\\\  & =\\frac{(-9)\\cdot 5+(-5)\\cdot 2+(-5)\\cdot 1}{{5}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{-60\\,}{\\,\\,\\,30\\,}b \\\\  & =-2b \\\\  & =-2[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "activity-ff338657-7250-5578-bcc8-03bd435ba33a" :activity/id #uuid "ff338657-7250-5578-bcc8-03bd435ba33a" :activity/title "Projecting Vectors Onto One-Dimensional Subspaces" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048893 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-42d6a83e-c70e-50e5-a15f-f59fe7b2b27f" "step-9b0f69b0-253f-59a9-ae75-3ab70a053b30" "step-0a7c3978-56f8-5be3-997f-dd9a4f1bdf46" "step-ed883a17-ffee-595d-8044-92cfccdf6e88" "step-33038e04-8255-5530-ac72-35d4ad10ba81"] :activity/first-step "step-42d6a83e-c70e-50e5-a15f-f59fe7b2b27f" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-42d6a83e-c70e-50e5-a15f-f59fe7b2b27f" :step/id #uuid "42d6a83e-c70e-50e5-a15f-f59fe7b2b27f" :step/content 17592186417871 :step/next "step-9b0f69b0-253f-59a9-ae75-3ab70a053b30" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-9b0f69b0-253f-59a9-ae75-3ab70a053b30" :step/id #uuid "9b0f69b0-253f-59a9-ae75-3ab70a053b30" :step/content 17592186326840 :step/next "step-0a7c3978-56f8-5be3-997f-dd9a4f1bdf46" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-0a7c3978-56f8-5be3-997f-dd9a4f1bdf46" :step/id #uuid "0a7c3978-56f8-5be3-997f-dd9a4f1bdf46" :step/content 17592186134288 :step/next "step-ed883a17-ffee-595d-8044-92cfccdf6e88" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-ed883a17-ffee-595d-8044-92cfccdf6e88" :step/id #uuid "ed883a17-ffee-595d-8044-92cfccdf6e88" :step/content 17592186394140 :step/next "step-33038e04-8255-5530-ac72-35d4ad10ba81" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-33038e04-8255-5530-ac72-35d4ad10ba81" :step/id #uuid "33038e04-8255-5530-ac72-35d4ad10ba81" :step/content 17592186417869 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179814 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2d4d7236-1971-52db-b086-a77353f5fe00"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179939 :question/problem "$3-5.8=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $5.8$ is larger than $3,$ the answer will be negative.\n\nWe start by solving an easier problem, which is to carry out the subtraction in the reverse order:\n\n$$\n5.8-3=2.8\n$$\n\nHowever, since our answer must be negative, we need to change the sign of the answer above. \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n3-5.8=-2.8\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186177303 :answer-field/correct 17592186177308 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-2d4d7236-1971-52db-b086-a77353f5fe00" :question/id #uuid "2d4d7236-1971-52db-b086-a77353f5fe00" :question/math-academy-id "q-128150" :question/problem "$6-8.7=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $8.7$ is larger than $6,$ the answer will be negative.\n\nWe start by solving an easier problem, which is to carry out the subtraction in the reverse order:\n\n$$\n8.7-6=2.7\n$$\n\nHowever, since our answer must be negative, we need to change the sign of the answer above. \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n6-8.7=-2.7\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-256bfffe-3c5a-55f0-9609-ab2ee806e2e9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-256bfffe-3c5a-55f0-9609-ab2ee806e2e9" :answer-field/id #uuid "256bfffe-3c5a-55f0-9609-ab2ee806e2e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b7bebb8b-c4ab-5271-bbaa-eee64c1d8059" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2862e84e-f462-5032-8dfa-808b3125fdc2" "answer-823212ce-1cfb-5c75-bb92-ea01b8f7d8f0" "answer-de59e0c6-68e6-5941-9e8b-b9a2293c2c62" "answer-41078b77-96a0-5f7c-bb94-1be5ec73d85e" "answer-b7bebb8b-c4ab-5271-bbaa-eee64c1d8059"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2862e84e-f462-5032-8dfa-808b3125fdc2" :answer/id #uuid "2862e84e-f462-5032-8dfa-808b3125fdc2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1.3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-823212ce-1cfb-5c75-bb92-ea01b8f7d8f0" :answer/id #uuid "823212ce-1cfb-5c75-bb92-ea01b8f7d8f0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1.7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-de59e0c6-68e6-5941-9e8b-b9a2293c2c62" :answer/id #uuid "de59e0c6-68e6-5941-9e8b-b9a2293c2c62" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-14.7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-41078b77-96a0-5f7c-bb94-1be5ec73d85e" :answer/id #uuid "41078b77-96a0-5f7c-bb94-1be5ec73d85e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2.3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b7bebb8b-c4ab-5271-bbaa-eee64c1d8059" :answer/id #uuid "b7bebb8b-c4ab-5271-bbaa-eee64c1d8059" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2.7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179813 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8f920e3a-0555-530e-885c-0505096145f6"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-8f920e3a-0555-530e-885c-0505096145f6" :question/id #uuid "8f920e3a-0555-530e-885c-0505096145f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-186102" :question/problem "$-3.6-2.3=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The answer will be negative since $-3.6$ is negative, and we're subtracting $2.3$ from it.\n\nWe start by solving an easier problem, which is to add their absolute values:\n\n$$\n3.6+2.3=5.9\n$$\n\nHowever, since our answer must be negative, we need to change the sign of the answer above. \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n-3.6-2.3=-5.9.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-89d68d1f-65d8-579a-bcd1-d6d181a6133c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-89d68d1f-65d8-579a-bcd1-d6d181a6133c" :answer-field/id #uuid "89d68d1f-65d8-579a-bcd1-d6d181a6133c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-f51a08b5-544d-56bf-b524-00c4e402e1fe" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f51a08b5-544d-56bf-b524-00c4e402e1fe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f51a08b5-544d-56bf-b524-00c4e402e1fe" :answer/id #uuid "f51a08b5-544d-56bf-b524-00c4e402e1fe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5.9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-1faba002-0fe8-5c3a-b172-4c31ffabe97e" :activity/id #uuid "1faba002-0fe8-5c3a-b172-4c31ffabe97e" :activity/title "Subtracting a Positive Decimal From a Smaller Decimal" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048207 :activity/course 17592186046648 :activity/steps ["step-27274a67-7e81-5979-85d8-f958ecda56fb" "step-f559c8d7-8fa9-505d-b2c5-2de1201ff6c8" "step-44f0d32e-1ab0-51b5-842a-9748e41b55ab"] :activity/first-step "step-27274a67-7e81-5979-85d8-f958ecda56fb" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-27274a67-7e81-5979-85d8-f958ecda56fb" :step/id #uuid "27274a67-7e81-5979-85d8-f958ecda56fb" :step/content 17592186179939 :step/next "step-f559c8d7-8fa9-505d-b2c5-2de1201ff6c8" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-f559c8d7-8fa9-505d-b2c5-2de1201ff6c8" :step/id #uuid "f559c8d7-8fa9-505d-b2c5-2de1201ff6c8" :step/content "question-2d4d7236-1971-52db-b086-a77353f5fe00" :step/next "step-44f0d32e-1ab0-51b5-842a-9748e41b55ab" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-44f0d32e-1ab0-51b5-842a-9748e41b55ab" :step/id #uuid "44f0d32e-1ab0-51b5-842a-9748e41b55ab" :step/content "question-8f920e3a-0555-530e-885c-0505096145f6" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054711 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-becc645b-7ce0-5458-abaa-ac04d1cbd85e" "question-a6b20681-3c0e-56d8-a0d2-12a0033bb72d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-becc645b-7ce0-5458-abaa-ac04d1cbd85e" :question/id #uuid "becc645b-7ce0-5458-abaa-ac04d1cbd85e" :question/math-academy-id "q-51576" :question/problem "Classify the polynomial $5{y}^{3}-2y+1$ according to its degree and the number of terms." :question/worked-solution "The polynomial $5{y}^{3}-2y+1$ has three terms, so it is a trinomial.\n\nAlso, its degree is $3,$ so it is a cubic.\n\nTherefore, the polynomial is a cubic trinomial." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-61ac3cf1-ce85-58c6-8c68-c0b075726ad0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-61ac3cf1-ce85-58c6-8c68-c0b075726ad0" :answer-field/id #uuid "61ac3cf1-ce85-58c6-8c68-c0b075726ad0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b5c26456-8a47-5d01-856e-a94ad2152272" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8ee88caf-fa42-587f-9b4e-37e2a9c5dac2" "answer-b5c26456-8a47-5d01-856e-a94ad2152272" "answer-1056a69e-6f45-5662-8064-9d31764878d8" "answer-71053b83-643d-5ef2-8a38-901d38fcd2cf" "answer-683a7cea-5988-5760-91b7-9df2a330db1a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8ee88caf-fa42-587f-9b4e-37e2a9c5dac2" :answer/id #uuid "8ee88caf-fa42-587f-9b4e-37e2a9c5dac2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Cubic binomial" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b5c26456-8a47-5d01-856e-a94ad2152272" :answer/id #uuid "b5c26456-8a47-5d01-856e-a94ad2152272" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Cubic trinomial" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1056a69e-6f45-5662-8064-9d31764878d8" :answer/id #uuid "1056a69e-6f45-5662-8064-9d31764878d8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Quadratic trinomial" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-71053b83-643d-5ef2-8a38-901d38fcd2cf" :answer/id #uuid "71053b83-643d-5ef2-8a38-901d38fcd2cf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Quartic binomial" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-683a7cea-5988-5760-91b7-9df2a330db1a" :answer/id #uuid "683a7cea-5988-5760-91b7-9df2a330db1a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Quartic trinomial" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a6b20681-3c0e-56d8-a0d2-12a0033bb72d" :question/id #uuid "a6b20681-3c0e-56d8-a0d2-12a0033bb72d" :question/math-academy-id "q-111223" :question/problem "Categorize the polynomial ${z}^{5}-3z$ based on its degree and the number of terms it has." :question/worked-solution "The polynomial ${z}^{5}-3z$ has two terms, so it is a binomial.\n\nAlso, its degree is $5,$ so it is a quintic.\n\nTherefore, the polynomial is a quintic binomial." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5cfb5c23-969b-59ed-8fd1-dd67557eb34a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5cfb5c23-969b-59ed-8fd1-dd67557eb34a" :answer-field/id #uuid "5cfb5c23-969b-59ed-8fd1-dd67557eb34a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3a6cf1b6-04ad-59dd-b257-00f1e1dce62c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-44e3a524-e613-5db6-91fb-801225462723" "answer-1d6c22d6-e892-5446-9098-76a0b21515ab" "answer-32c65879-0672-5962-bea0-b0afdace1faf" "answer-8caae5ad-2091-5a94-9de8-16bbe05ea834" "answer-3a6cf1b6-04ad-59dd-b257-00f1e1dce62c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-44e3a524-e613-5db6-91fb-801225462723" :answer/id #uuid "44e3a524-e613-5db6-91fb-801225462723" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "cubic trinomial" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1d6c22d6-e892-5446-9098-76a0b21515ab" :answer/id #uuid "1d6c22d6-e892-5446-9098-76a0b21515ab" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "linear binomial" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-32c65879-0672-5962-bea0-b0afdace1faf" :answer/id #uuid "32c65879-0672-5962-bea0-b0afdace1faf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "quadratic trinomial" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8caae5ad-2091-5a94-9de8-16bbe05ea834" :answer/id #uuid "8caae5ad-2091-5a94-9de8-16bbe05ea834" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "quartic binomial" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a6cf1b6-04ad-59dd-b257-00f1e1dce62c" :answer/id #uuid "3a6cf1b6-04ad-59dd-b257-00f1e1dce62c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "quintic binomial" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054712 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4a6f2377-d91b-5967-99d3-f6f62abbce5f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-4a6f2377-d91b-5967-99d3-f6f62abbce5f" :question/id #uuid "4a6f2377-d91b-5967-99d3-f6f62abbce5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-152221" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. The sum of $4{x}^{2}+1$ and $2{x}^{2}$ is a binomial.\n\nII. The sum of $3{x}^{2}-2x$ and $5$ is a binomial.\n\nIII. The sum of a binomial and a monomial is **sometimes** a binomial." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n(4{x}^{2}+1)+(2{x}^{2})=6{x}^{2}+1,\n$$\n\nwhich is a binomial.\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n(3{x}^{2}-2x)+(5)=3{x}^{2}-2x+5,\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a binomial (it's a trinomial).\n\n- Statement III is true. The sum of a binomial and a monomial *could be* a binomial (see, for example, statement I). \n\n However, the sum of a binomial and a monomial is *not always* a binomial (see, for example, statement II). \n\n So, the sum of a binomial and a monomial is *sometimes* binomial.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9a727332-6125-5bf9-8eee-55ebc8cbefb8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9a727332-6125-5bf9-8eee-55ebc8cbefb8" :answer-field/id #uuid "9a727332-6125-5bf9-8eee-55ebc8cbefb8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-61b11b3a-81a6-51e7-8ad5-0ea10ae35041" :answer-field/choices ["answer-61b11b3a-81a6-51e7-8ad5-0ea10ae35041" "answer-e4b1e4de-0805-5bfa-8e45-f45979420ad8" "answer-f7a93d32-18e7-5f8e-961d-886b53773bcf" "answer-7d3ca694-1f66-5b36-b822-4d155549fa8d" "answer-a66f3f1f-29ec-5be2-8bc5-722c8e836b92"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-61b11b3a-81a6-51e7-8ad5-0ea10ae35041" :answer/id #uuid "61b11b3a-81a6-51e7-8ad5-0ea10ae35041" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4b1e4de-0805-5bfa-8e45-f45979420ad8" :answer/id #uuid "e4b1e4de-0805-5bfa-8e45-f45979420ad8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f7a93d32-18e7-5f8e-961d-886b53773bcf" :answer/id #uuid "f7a93d32-18e7-5f8e-961d-886b53773bcf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d3ca694-1f66-5b36-b822-4d155549fa8d" :answer/id #uuid "7d3ca694-1f66-5b36-b822-4d155549fa8d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a66f3f1f-29ec-5be2-8bc5-722c8e836b92" :answer/id #uuid "a66f3f1f-29ec-5be2-8bc5-722c8e836b92" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-9d9c15c2-a381-503f-8c20-095ae4a1ed50" :activity/id #uuid "9d9c15c2-a381-503f-8c20-095ae4a1ed50" :activity/title "Monomials, Binomials and Trinomials" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048548 :activity/course 17592186046648 :activity/steps ["step-05338efe-276c-51da-8655-6d896721903f" "step-39e3b333-15cb-5985-99ff-5688251c0604" "step-33040ec4-d764-5460-b879-febde2296a30"] :activity/first-step "step-05338efe-276c-51da-8655-6d896721903f" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-05338efe-276c-51da-8655-6d896721903f" :step/id #uuid "05338efe-276c-51da-8655-6d896721903f" :step/content "question-becc645b-7ce0-5458-abaa-ac04d1cbd85e" :step/next "step-39e3b333-15cb-5985-99ff-5688251c0604" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-39e3b333-15cb-5985-99ff-5688251c0604" :step/id #uuid "39e3b333-15cb-5985-99ff-5688251c0604" :step/content "question-a6b20681-3c0e-56d8-a0d2-12a0033bb72d" :step/next "step-33040ec4-d764-5460-b879-febde2296a30" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-33040ec4-d764-5460-b879-febde2296a30" :step/id #uuid "33040ec4-d764-5460-b879-febde2296a30" :step/content "question-4a6f2377-d91b-5967-99d3-f6f62abbce5f" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186134521 :question/answer-fields 17592186133164] {:db/id 17592186392340 :question/worked-solution "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ \nAnd, since the set $\\{v,w\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{3\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 1}{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}v=\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{3\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}w=\\frac{11}{6}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{11}{6}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(4[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+11[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134262 :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\},$ $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\},$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{3}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{7}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1\\cdot 3+4\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{11}{12}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{3}}{{a}_{3}\\cdot {a}_{3}}{a}_{3}=\\frac{1\\cdot 0+4\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=-\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{11}{12}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(21[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+11[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-8[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186351311 :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\},$ $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\},$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{3}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{0\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 0+(-3)\\cdot 2+(-12)\\cdot (-2)}{(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{0\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 0+(-3)\\cdot 2+(-12)\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{3}}{{a}_{3}\\cdot {a}_{3}}{a}_{3}=\\frac{0\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 0+(-3)\\cdot (-1)+(-12)\\cdot (-2)}{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134521 :question/worked-solution "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ \nAnd, since the set $\\{v,w\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{10\\cdot (-2)+6\\cdot 1+14\\cdot 3}{(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}v=2[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{10\\cdot 2+6\\cdot (-2)+14\\cdot 2}{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}}w=3[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186272027 :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\},$ $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\},$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{3}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{7\\cdot 1+3\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 0}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{7\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{a}_{2}=3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{3}}{{a}_{3}\\cdot {a}_{3}}{a}_{3}=\\frac{7\\cdot (-3)+3\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 2}{(-3)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{a}_{2}=-[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+(-1)[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "activity-78343401-39ba-5fb3-a589-44e165c5cb6e" :activity/id #uuid "78343401-39ba-5fb3-a589-44e165c5cb6e" :activity/title "Projecting Vectors Onto Subspaces in Euclidean Spaces (Orthogonal Bases)" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050009 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-a4cae37a-e3ec-588f-b8a6-214967984746" "step-ec0f94d1-ae62-5f49-8d28-cff1d955f3ae" "step-ac5df48c-0726-5423-8c97-7412ea5b845f" "step-ec33a571-16a5-5a2d-8028-b38ef864240e" "step-f3b630ff-474b-5c65-b9cb-a6f7547e51ad"] :activity/first-step "step-a4cae37a-e3ec-588f-b8a6-214967984746" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-a4cae37a-e3ec-588f-b8a6-214967984746" :step/id #uuid "a4cae37a-e3ec-588f-b8a6-214967984746" :step/content 17592186392340 :step/next "step-ec0f94d1-ae62-5f49-8d28-cff1d955f3ae" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-ec0f94d1-ae62-5f49-8d28-cff1d955f3ae" :step/id #uuid "ec0f94d1-ae62-5f49-8d28-cff1d955f3ae" :step/content 17592186134262 :step/next "step-ac5df48c-0726-5423-8c97-7412ea5b845f" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-ac5df48c-0726-5423-8c97-7412ea5b845f" :step/id #uuid "ac5df48c-0726-5423-8c97-7412ea5b845f" :step/content 17592186351311 :step/next "step-ec33a571-16a5-5a2d-8028-b38ef864240e" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-ec33a571-16a5-5a2d-8028-b38ef864240e" :step/id #uuid "ec33a571-16a5-5a2d-8028-b38ef864240e" :step/content 17592186134521 :step/next "step-f3b630ff-474b-5c65-b9cb-a6f7547e51ad" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-f3b630ff-474b-5c65-b9cb-a6f7547e51ad" :step/id #uuid "f3b630ff-474b-5c65-b9cb-a6f7547e51ad" :step/content 17592186272027 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186184197 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-dfba6eb1-d0b6-5cbc-ac16-d682a2e1cc93"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186184095 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. Any $1\\times4$ matrix $A$ has at most $1$ singular value.\n\nII. Any $4\\times2$ matrix $A$ has at most $2$ distinct singular values.\n\nIII. The matrix ![](images/b8/b8601b1e66ef1d302e7194f262f6a513063bba2114fb063c2fc16542d4684392.svg) has one negative singular value." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-dfba6eb1-d0b6-5cbc-ac16-d682a2e1cc93" :question/id #uuid "dfba6eb1-d0b6-5cbc-ac16-d682a2e1cc93" :question/math-academy-id "q-95286" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. Any $6\\times 5$ matrix $A$ has at most $6$ distinct singular values.\n\nII. Any $8\\times 9$ matrix $A$ has at most $9$ distinct singular values.\n\nIII. The matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ has at most two non-negative distinct singular values." :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $A$ is a $6\\times 5$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $5\\times 6$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $5\\times 5$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{5\\times 6}\\times {A}_{6\\times 5}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{5\\times 5}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $5$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since $A$ is a $8\\times 9$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $9\\times 8$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $9\\times 9$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{9\\times 8}\\times {A}_{8\\times 9}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{9\\times 9}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $9$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement III is true.  Indeed, all singular values of any matrix are non-negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6457d0d0-1968-5324-849a-7bcaae3a3d7b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6457d0d0-1968-5324-849a-7bcaae3a3d7b" :answer-field/id #uuid "6457d0d0-1968-5324-849a-7bcaae3a3d7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-21c0505d-1159-59fb-ba8b-2bd5ac4199de" :answer-field/choices ["answer-08b31b5f-4d45-56bb-9d27-10f181056843" "answer-e4ada2c6-ebf0-5d58-9a21-8f0d7d2bddb7" "answer-1f5ebfbd-349c-5710-ab1d-bffdb6d4d025" "answer-21c0505d-1159-59fb-ba8b-2bd5ac4199de" "answer-94c66a12-70a7-529f-ba21-ac7f034b13c8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-08b31b5f-4d45-56bb-9d27-10f181056843" :answer/id #uuid "08b31b5f-4d45-56bb-9d27-10f181056843" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4ada2c6-ebf0-5d58-9a21-8f0d7d2bddb7" :answer/id #uuid "e4ada2c6-ebf0-5d58-9a21-8f0d7d2bddb7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1f5ebfbd-349c-5710-ab1d-bffdb6d4d025" :answer/id #uuid "1f5ebfbd-349c-5710-ab1d-bffdb6d4d025" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-21c0505d-1159-59fb-ba8b-2bd5ac4199de" :answer/id #uuid "21c0505d-1159-59fb-ba8b-2bd5ac4199de" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-94c66a12-70a7-529f-ba21-ac7f034b13c8" :answer/id #uuid "94c66a12-70a7-529f-ba21-ac7f034b13c8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186220231 :answer-field/choices 17592186220232] [:db/retract 17592186220231 :answer-field/choices 17592186220233] [:db/retract 17592186220231 :answer-field/choices 17592186220234] [:db/retract 17592186220231 :answer-field/choices 17592186220235] [:db/retract 17592186220231 :answer-field/choices 17592186220236] {:db/id 17592186221939 :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{1}{s(s-3)},\\,s>3\n$$\n\nfind the inverse Laplace transform of $G(s),$ given that\n$L\\{{e}^{at}\\}=\\frac{1}{s-a},\\text{ }s>a.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can write $G(s)$ as \n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{1}{s(s-3)}=\\frac{F(s)}{s},\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=\\frac{1}{s-3}.$\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\nL\\{{e}^{at}\\}=\\frac{1}{s-a}.\n$$\n\nThus, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\} & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-3}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we know that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nUsing the property above in reverse, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}\\{G(s)\\} & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{F(s)}{s}\\} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{3τ}\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3τ}\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({e}^{3t}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186220231 :answer-field/correct "answer-31498a6a-f3af-5dd5-be6d-11d44f51fea5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-44996372-2e93-5895-8ff0-6abd990cc4c4" "answer-31498a6a-f3af-5dd5-be6d-11d44f51fea5" "answer-a31be869-7c0d-5e9e-bdcc-81854a7a612c" "answer-2a287c4c-314b-5a3d-a1a5-ee8569803cd1" "answer-ae8e2fbf-32b7-580c-ac00-6c30112bdac2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-44996372-2e93-5895-8ff0-6abd990cc4c4" :answer/id #uuid "44996372-2e93-5895-8ff0-6abd990cc4c4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}(1-{e}^{3t})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-31498a6a-f3af-5dd5-be6d-11d44f51fea5" :answer/id #uuid "31498a6a-f3af-5dd5-be6d-11d44f51fea5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}({e}^{3t}-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a31be869-7c0d-5e9e-bdcc-81854a7a612c" :answer/id #uuid "a31be869-7c0d-5e9e-bdcc-81854a7a612c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}({e}^{t}-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2a287c4c-314b-5a3d-a1a5-ee8569803cd1" :answer/id #uuid "2a287c4c-314b-5a3d-a1a5-ee8569803cd1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t-3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae8e2fbf-32b7-580c-ac00-6c30112bdac2" :answer/id #uuid "ae8e2fbf-32b7-580c-ac00-6c30112bdac2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{3t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186304857 :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{24}{{s}^{6}},\\,s>0\n$$\n\nfind the inverse Laplace transform of $G(s),$ given that\n$L\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can write $G(s)$ as \n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{24}{{s}^{6}}=\\frac{F(s)}{s},\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=\\frac{24}{{s}^{5}}.$\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\nL\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\n$$\n\nThus, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\} & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{24}{{s}^{5}}\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{4!}{{s}^{4+1}}\\} \\\\  & ={t}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we know that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nUsing the property above in reverse, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}\\{G(s)\\} & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{F(s)}{s}\\} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}{τ}^{4}\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186304859 :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{1}{s(s-5)},\\,s>5\n$$\n\nfind the inverse Laplace transform of $G(s),$ given that\n$L\\{{e}^{at}\\}=\\frac{1}{s-a},\\text{ }s>a.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can write $G(s)$ as \n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{1}{s(s-5)}=\\frac{F(s)}{s},\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=\\frac{1}{s-5}.$\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\nL\\{{e}^{at}\\}=\\frac{1}{s-a}.\n$$\n\nThus, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\} & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-5}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{5t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we know that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nUsing the property above in reverse, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}\\{G(s)\\} & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{F(s)}{s}\\} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{5τ}\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{5τ}\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}({e}^{5t}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186370100 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find $L\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ\\}$ given $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nLet $A(t)$ be the anti-derivative of $f(t)$ such that ${A}^{′}(t)=f(t)$ and $A(0)=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ={{field-1}}\\,{|}_{0}^{t}=A(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ\\}=L\\{A(t)\\}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}A(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's use integration by parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=A(t) & ⇒ & du={A}^{′}(t)\\text{ }dt \\\\ dv={{field-3}} & ⇒ & v=-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt & =A(t)(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st})\\,{|}_{0}^{\\infty }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }({{field-4}}){A}^{′}(t)\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{1}{s}A(t){e}^{-st}{|}_{0}^{\\infty }=$ {{field-5}}, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt=\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nFinally, using that ${A}^{′}(t)=f(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-6}}\\,dt & =\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A(t)$ be the anti-derivative of $f(t)$ such that ${A}^{′}(t)=f(t)$ and $A(0)=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ & =A(τ)\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =A(t)-A(0) \\\\  & =A(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\} & =L\\{A(t)\\}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's use integration by parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=A(t) & \\,⇒\\, & du={A}^{′}(t)\\,dt \\\\ dv={e}^{-st}\\,dt & ⇒ & v=-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt & =A(t)(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st})\\,{|}_{0}^{\\infty }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}){A}^{′}(t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A(0)=0$ and $\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have $-\\frac{1}{s}A(t){e}^{-st}{|}_{0}^{\\infty }=0$ and\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt=\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nFinally, using that ${A}^{′}(t)=f(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt & =\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "activity-246acae1-e869-5cdc-82eb-aa3a36e73741" :activity/id #uuid "246acae1-e869-5cdc-82eb-aa3a36e73741" :activity/title "Laplace Transforms of Integrals" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049050 :activity/course 17592186046660 :activity/steps ["step-d4588b0a-e26c-5506-9b19-98aa6f45486d" "step-3298c911-4b68-58bb-83be-c53fead7b7b9" "step-4e4e4c22-0f71-57e6-bb91-c83cf64a6cfe" "step-1157fa95-5ca0-5552-8548-4e37f9ab0aa7"] :activity/first-step "step-d4588b0a-e26c-5506-9b19-98aa6f45486d" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-d4588b0a-e26c-5506-9b19-98aa6f45486d" :step/id #uuid "d4588b0a-e26c-5506-9b19-98aa6f45486d" :step/content 17592186221939 :step/next "step-3298c911-4b68-58bb-83be-c53fead7b7b9" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-3298c911-4b68-58bb-83be-c53fead7b7b9" :step/id #uuid "3298c911-4b68-58bb-83be-c53fead7b7b9" :step/content 17592186304857 :step/next "step-4e4e4c22-0f71-57e6-bb91-c83cf64a6cfe" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-4e4e4c22-0f71-57e6-bb91-c83cf64a6cfe" :step/id #uuid "4e4e4c22-0f71-57e6-bb91-c83cf64a6cfe" :step/content 17592186304859 :step/next "step-1157fa95-5ca0-5552-8548-4e37f9ab0aa7" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-1157fa95-5ca0-5552-8548-4e37f9ab0aa7" :step/id #uuid "1157fa95-5ca0-5552-8548-4e37f9ab0aa7" :step/content 17592186370100 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087709 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-61b55285-bdfc-5092-a032-79dc8a8f2ea3" "question-c460c44e-72fe-595b-9fd7-cf8fb6438c3d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087437 :question/problem "Consider the equations of the following lines.\n\nI. $-5x + y=3$\n\nII. $3x - y= -2$\n\nIII. $3x= 5$\n\nIV. $2x- 6y= 4$\n\nWhich of these lines is written in standard form?" :question/worked-solution "The *standard form* equation of a line is \n\n$$\nax+by=c,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ are all integers,\n\n- $a\\geq 0,$ and\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ have no common factors greater than $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equations.\n\n- Equation I is *not* in standard form because the coefficient of $x$ is negative ($-5$).\n\n- Equation II is in standard form. Indeed, the variables are on the left-hand side, while the constant term is on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{3x- y}_{\\textrm{variables}} \\quad = \\underbrace{\\phantom{y}-2\\phantom{y}}_{\\textrm{constant}}\n$$\n\n \nAll coefficients are integers (namely, $3,$ $-1,$ and $-2$) and don't have common factors greater than $1.$ Also, the coefficient of $x$ is positive ($3$).\n\n- Equation III is in standard form. Indeed, the variables are on the left-hand side, while the constant term is on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{3x}_{\\textrm{variables}} \\quad = \\underbrace{\\phantom{y}5\\phantom{y}}_{\\textrm{constant}}\n$$\n\n \nAll coefficients are integers (namely, $3$ and $5$) and don't have common factors greater than $1.$ Also, the coefficient of $x$ is positive ($3$).\n\n- Equation IV is *not* in standard form because the coefficients $(2,-6,$ and $4)$ have a common factor of $2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087710 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9b7addf0-160c-54bc-8147-e2a24b57e5bd" "question-8d0eef6f-b433-56d1-a89d-4aab29bca392"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087438 :question/worked-solution "Our goal is to write the equation in the form $ax+by = c,$ where $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\geq 0.$\n\nWe begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =7x+\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ y-7x & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ -7x+y & =\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow the equation is in the form $ax+by=c.$ However, it violates the rule $a \\geq 0.$ We can fix this by multiplying the equation by $-1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7x+y & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-7x+y) & =(-1)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\ 7x-y & =-\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $c$ is a fraction, not an integer. To fix this, we multiply by $5$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x-y & =-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 5\\cdot (7x-y) & =5\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\ 35x-5y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\geq 0.$ So we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is $35x-5y=-2.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087711 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4c641ffa-5f29-5085-9bd8-14bb6fe11280" "question-62452c7f-a9e2-5c51-81ef-baa2650df43c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087439 :question/worked-solution "Our goal is to write the equation in the form $ax+by = c,$ where $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors and $a\\geq 0.$\n\nWe begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n-x+y=1.\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply by $-1$ to make the $x$-coefficient positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+y & =1 \\\\ (-1)(-x+y) & =-1 \\\\ x-y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a \\geq 0.$ So we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is $x-y=-1.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087712 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-03ad1173-6dbd-512f-86a7-67df49248ff3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087440 :question/problem "Calculate the equation of the straight line that passes through the points $(-1,3)$ and $(2,4).$ Write the answer in standard form." :question/worked-solution "First, we will construct the point-slope form of the line. Then, we will simplify it into standard form.\n\nTo construct the point-slope form of the line, we need a point and a slope. We already have two points, so we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{4-3}{2-(-1)}=\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use this slope along with one of the points to write the point-slope form equation of the line. It doesn't matter which point we choose, but let's choose $(2,4).$ The point-slope equation is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo simplify the point-slope formula into standard form, we first multiply by $3$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-4 & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-2) \\\\ 3\\cdot (y-4) & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(x-2) \\\\ 3y-12 & =x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we move the variables to the left side and the constant to the right side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y-12 & =x-2 \\\\ 3y & =x+10 \\\\ -x+3y & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply by $-1$ to make the $x$-coefficient positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+3y & =10 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-x+3y) & =(-1)\\cdot 10 \\\\ x-3y & =-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the standard form of the equation is $x-3y = -10.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-61b55285-bdfc-5092-a032-79dc8a8f2ea3" :question/id #uuid "61b55285-bdfc-5092-a032-79dc8a8f2ea3" :question/math-academy-id "q-216934" :question/problem "Consider the equations of the following lines.\n\nI. $3y=5$\n\nII. $-x+7y=0$\n\nIII. $5x-3y=6$\n\nWhich of these lines is written in standard form?" :question/worked-solution "The *standard form* equation of a line is \n\n$$\nax+by=c,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ are all integers,\n\n- $a\\ge 0,$ and\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ have no common factors greater than $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equations.\n\n- Equation I is in standard form. Indeed, the variables are on the left-hand side, while the constant term is on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n{{3y}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variables}}\\,={{5}_{⏟}}_{\\text{constant}}\n$$\n\n \nAll coefficients are integers (namely, $3$ and $5$) and don't have common factors greater than $1.$ Also, the coefficient of $x$ is non-negative ($0$).\n\n- Equation II is *not* in standard form because the coefficient of $x$ is negative ($-1$).\n\n- Equation III is in standard form. Indeed, the variables are on the left-hand side, while the constant term is on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n{{5x-3y}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variables}}\\,={{6}_{⏟}}_{\\text{constant}}\n$$\n\n \nAll coefficients are integers (namely, $5,$ $-3,$ and $6$) and don't have common factors greater than $1.$ Also, the coefficient of $x$ is positive ($5$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-867602d4-5dfd-58fb-9675-72a40d72afbb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-867602d4-5dfd-58fb-9675-72a40d72afbb" :answer-field/id #uuid "867602d4-5dfd-58fb-9675-72a40d72afbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-26f25391-da6a-50f7-9fbb-57467d22e097" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9ee14cb3-89bd-5f29-ae17-4f05933c4fde" "answer-26f25391-da6a-50f7-9fbb-57467d22e097" "answer-1bbe4881-0890-557a-94a3-38fa413a495b" "answer-319cea52-16bc-509f-b234-b5a50371b20c" "answer-288dfc3c-caee-5e89-b10d-3410df396b1d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9ee14cb3-89bd-5f29-ae17-4f05933c4fde" :answer/id #uuid "9ee14cb3-89bd-5f29-ae17-4f05933c4fde" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-26f25391-da6a-50f7-9fbb-57467d22e097" :answer/id #uuid "26f25391-da6a-50f7-9fbb-57467d22e097" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1bbe4881-0890-557a-94a3-38fa413a495b" :answer/id #uuid "1bbe4881-0890-557a-94a3-38fa413a495b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-319cea52-16bc-509f-b234-b5a50371b20c" :answer/id #uuid "319cea52-16bc-509f-b234-b5a50371b20c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-288dfc3c-caee-5e89-b10d-3410df396b1d" :answer/id #uuid "288dfc3c-caee-5e89-b10d-3410df396b1d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c460c44e-72fe-595b-9fd7-cf8fb6438c3d" :question/id #uuid "c460c44e-72fe-595b-9fd7-cf8fb6438c3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-216943" :question/problem "Consider the equations of the following lines.\n\nI. $4x+2y=14$\n\nII. $5x=10$\n\nIII. $5x-3y=1$\n\nWhich of these equations are written in standard form?" :question/worked-solution "The *standard form* equation of a line is \n\n$$\nax+by=c,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ are all integers,\n\n- $a\\ge 0,$ and\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ have no common factors greater than $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equations in turn.\n\n- Equation I is *not* in standard form because the coefficients $(4,2,$ and $14)$ have a common factor of $2.$\n\n- Equation II is *not* in standard form because the coefficients $(5$ and $10)$ have a common factor of $5.$\n\n- Equation III is in standard form. Indeed, the variables are on the left-hand side, while the constant term is on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n{{5x-3y}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variables}}\\,={{1}_{⏟}}_{\\text{constant}}\n$$\n\n \nAll coefficients are integers (namely, $5,$ $-3,$ and $1$) and don't have common factors greater than $1.$ Also, the coefficient of $x$ is positive ($5$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-873a5bb4-ef75-574c-a0d5-064e793e8973"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-873a5bb4-ef75-574c-a0d5-064e793e8973" :answer-field/id #uuid "873a5bb4-ef75-574c-a0d5-064e793e8973" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b08de677-8ae6-519e-9dd4-3f754943d262" :answer-field/choices ["answer-eea176cd-3d65-5d28-9d12-6524ebb071c2" "answer-8226540d-9c79-5a28-b1a1-75e03dfe607d" "answer-f1404a4a-a824-58ce-b097-efdd72add08c" "answer-b08de677-8ae6-519e-9dd4-3f754943d262" "answer-77bca3b3-3cec-585b-b9e9-d37688da6161"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eea176cd-3d65-5d28-9d12-6524ebb071c2" :answer/id #uuid "eea176cd-3d65-5d28-9d12-6524ebb071c2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8226540d-9c79-5a28-b1a1-75e03dfe607d" :answer/id #uuid "8226540d-9c79-5a28-b1a1-75e03dfe607d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f1404a4a-a824-58ce-b097-efdd72add08c" :answer/id #uuid "f1404a4a-a824-58ce-b097-efdd72add08c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b08de677-8ae6-519e-9dd4-3f754943d262" :answer/id #uuid "b08de677-8ae6-519e-9dd4-3f754943d262" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-77bca3b3-3cec-585b-b9e9-d37688da6161" :answer/id #uuid "77bca3b3-3cec-585b-b9e9-d37688da6161" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of these listed" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186088219 :question/problem "Consider the equations of the following lines.\n\nI. $2x=3$\n\nII. $-x+2y=3$\n\nIII. $x-2y=0$\n\nWhich of these lines is written in standard form?" :question/worked-solution "The *standard form* equation of a line is \n\n$$\nax+by=c,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ are all integers,\n\n- $a\\ge 0,$ and\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ have no common factors greater than $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equations.\n\n- Equation I is in standard form. Indeed, the variables are on the left-hand side, while the constant term is on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n{{2x}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variables}}\\,={{3}_{⏟}}_{\\text{constant}}\n$$\n\n \nAll coefficients are integers (namely, $2$ and $3$) and don't have common factors greater than $1.$ Also, the coefficient of $x$ is positive ($2$).\n\n- Equation II is *not* in standard form because the coefficient of $x$ is negative ($-1$).\n\n- Equation III is in standard form. Indeed, the variables are on the left-hand side, while the constant term is on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n{{x-2y}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variables}}\\,={{0}_{⏟}}_{\\text{constant}}\n$$\n\n \nAll coefficients are integers (namely, $1,$ $-2,$ and $0$) and don't have common factors greater than $1.$ Also, the coefficient of $x$ is positive ($1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186085968 :answer-field/correct 17592186085973 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-9b7addf0-160c-54bc-8147-e2a24b57e5bd" :question/id #uuid "9b7addf0-160c-54bc-8147-e2a24b57e5bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-109609" :question/problem "Write $y=-7x-4$ in standard form." :question/worked-solution "Our goal is to write the equation in the form $ax+by=c,$ where $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors and $a\\ge 0.$\n\nWe begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-7x-4 \\\\ 7x+y & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\ge 0.$ So we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is $7x+y=-4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e06d190d-4809-59ce-85bf-42f3c5088325"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e06d190d-4809-59ce-85bf-42f3c5088325" :answer-field/id #uuid "e06d190d-4809-59ce-85bf-42f3c5088325" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f556b952-f090-56d3-9d73-f0c358dec808" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f556b952-f090-56d3-9d73-f0c358dec808" "answer-da1160f6-b217-5f4e-8d61-347872e99bb8" "answer-491c54a5-c931-5a8c-9a9a-3c88b0327d98" "answer-ee1ed2e4-de83-508f-b030-4f5015eeb2bb" "answer-0cefc6ed-d53f-5610-b027-2f4d4f49fe33"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f556b952-f090-56d3-9d73-f0c358dec808" :answer/id #uuid "f556b952-f090-56d3-9d73-f0c358dec808" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7x+y=-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-da1160f6-b217-5f4e-8d61-347872e99bb8" :answer/id #uuid "da1160f6-b217-5f4e-8d61-347872e99bb8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7x=-y-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-491c54a5-c931-5a8c-9a9a-3c88b0327d98" :answer/id #uuid "491c54a5-c931-5a8c-9a9a-3c88b0327d98" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y+4=-7x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ee1ed2e4-de83-508f-b030-4f5015eeb2bb" :answer/id #uuid "ee1ed2e4-de83-508f-b030-4f5015eeb2bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y+7x+4=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0cefc6ed-d53f-5610-b027-2f4d4f49fe33" :answer/id #uuid "0cefc6ed-d53f-5610-b027-2f4d4f49fe33" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y-7x=-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8d0eef6f-b433-56d1-a89d-4aab29bca392" :question/id #uuid "8d0eef6f-b433-56d1-a89d-4aab29bca392" :question/math-academy-id "q-214594" :question/problem "Express the equation of the line $y=\\frac{2}{5}x-3$ in standard form.\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n$=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Our goal is to write the equation in the form $ax+by=c,$ where $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors and $a\\ge 0.$\n\nWe begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{2}{5}x+y=-3\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply both sides of the equation by $-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{5}x+y & =-3 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{5}x+y) & =(-1)\\cdot (-3) \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}x-y & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply both sides of the equation by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}x-y & =3 \\\\ 5\\cdot (\\frac{2}{5}x-y) & =5\\cdot (3) \\\\ 2x-5y & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\ge 0.$ So we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is \n\n$$\n2x-5y=15.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2578c7dc-df07-5c9f-9883-dccf1b4fa276" "answer-field-adee3ac3-8ecd-58c3-a665-8c5d902d4cdf"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2578c7dc-df07-5c9f-9883-dccf1b4fa276" :answer-field/id #uuid "2578c7dc-df07-5c9f-9883-dccf1b4fa276" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-bfa1ad90-bf21-5bd7-9c4a-e2c278e3e210" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bfa1ad90-bf21-5bd7-9c4a-e2c278e3e210"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bfa1ad90-bf21-5bd7-9c4a-e2c278e3e210" :answer/id #uuid "bfa1ad90-bf21-5bd7-9c4a-e2c278e3e210" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2x-5y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-adee3ac3-8ecd-58c3-a665-8c5d902d4cdf" :answer-field/id #uuid "adee3ac3-8ecd-58c3-a665-8c5d902d4cdf" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-1900311e-ec2f-5a19-8cfb-a76c9ae262ca" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1900311e-ec2f-5a19-8cfb-a76c9ae262ca"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1900311e-ec2f-5a19-8cfb-a76c9ae262ca" :answer/id #uuid "1900311e-ec2f-5a19-8cfb-a76c9ae262ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4c641ffa-5f29-5085-9bd8-14bb6fe11280" :question/id #uuid "4c641ffa-5f29-5085-9bd8-14bb6fe11280" :question/math-academy-id "q-109615" :question/problem "Write $3x=y+1$ in standard form." :question/worked-solution "Our goal is to write the equation in the form $ax+by=c,$ where $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors and $a\\ge 0.$\n\nWe begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n3x-y=1.\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\ge 0.$ So we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is $3x-y=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-35a19470-a5b0-5f3b-87c6-f21a25237e12"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-35a19470-a5b0-5f3b-87c6-f21a25237e12" :answer-field/id #uuid "35a19470-a5b0-5f3b-87c6-f21a25237e12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8206681f-ad95-56cc-9419-2800e67d09c7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8bb7d2ea-8334-5ee0-bae9-43918b279ce9" "answer-c1c31ec5-c907-5d2c-be84-13eac945c469" "answer-8206681f-ad95-56cc-9419-2800e67d09c7" "answer-97591881-5b3c-5e83-beb2-296f55b78078" "answer-06c553f0-8dd8-5f5b-837c-09549d9227e4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8bb7d2ea-8334-5ee0-bae9-43918b279ce9" :answer/id #uuid "8bb7d2ea-8334-5ee0-bae9-43918b279ce9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3x+y=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c1c31ec5-c907-5d2c-be84-13eac945c469" :answer/id #uuid "c1c31ec5-c907-5d2c-be84-13eac945c469" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3x-y-1=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8206681f-ad95-56cc-9419-2800e67d09c7" :answer/id #uuid "8206681f-ad95-56cc-9419-2800e67d09c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3x-y=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-97591881-5b3c-5e83-beb2-296f55b78078" :answer/id #uuid "97591881-5b3c-5e83-beb2-296f55b78078" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x-3y=3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-06c553f0-8dd8-5f5b-837c-09549d9227e4" :answer/id #uuid "06c553f0-8dd8-5f5b-837c-09549d9227e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=3x-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-62452c7f-a9e2-5c51-81ef-baa2650df43c" :question/id #uuid "62452c7f-a9e2-5c51-81ef-baa2650df43c" :question/math-academy-id "q-13339" :question/problem "Write $3y+3x+3=0$ in standard form." :question/worked-solution "Our goal is to write the equation in the form $ax+by=c,$ where $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors and $a\\ge 0.$\n\nWe begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y+3x+3 & =0 \\\\ 3x+3y & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow the equation is in the form $ax+by=c.$ However, $a,b,$ and $c$ have a common factor of $3.$ So, we divide by $3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+3y & =-3 \\\\ \\frac{3x+3y}{3} & =\\frac{-3}{3} \\\\ x+y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\ge 0.$ So we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is $x+y=-1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b236d4f2-5cba-5c8d-9d04-e7159dd85f16"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b236d4f2-5cba-5c8d-9d04-e7159dd85f16" :answer-field/id #uuid "b236d4f2-5cba-5c8d-9d04-e7159dd85f16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-378b3a35-e891-5069-bb1c-e487f7301f59" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b08504a0-1c63-5fae-b984-a8655b54bb8e" "answer-378b3a35-e891-5069-bb1c-e487f7301f59" "answer-475cbe15-cd02-5bb4-96cb-090594d20d74" "answer-cade5605-46b0-523d-82c7-ef5ae58a3a9c" "answer-76d02a01-1789-5535-985c-146ec53f929c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b08504a0-1c63-5fae-b984-a8655b54bb8e" :answer/id #uuid "b08504a0-1c63-5fae-b984-a8655b54bb8e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-x+y=-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-378b3a35-e891-5069-bb1c-e487f7301f59" :answer/id #uuid "378b3a35-e891-5069-bb1c-e487f7301f59" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+y=-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-475cbe15-cd02-5bb4-96cb-090594d20d74" :answer/id #uuid "475cbe15-cd02-5bb4-96cb-090594d20d74" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+y=-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cade5605-46b0-523d-82c7-ef5ae58a3a9c" :answer/id #uuid "cade5605-46b0-523d-82c7-ef5ae58a3a9c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+y=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-76d02a01-1789-5535-985c-146ec53f929c" :answer/id #uuid "76d02a01-1789-5535-985c-146ec53f929c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x-y=-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-03ad1173-6dbd-512f-86a7-67df49248ff3" :question/id #uuid "03ad1173-6dbd-512f-86a7-67df49248ff3" :question/math-academy-id "q-109627" :question/problem "What is the equation of the line that passes through the points $(-5,5)$ and $(-1,6)$, expressed in standard form?" :question/worked-solution "First, we will construct the point-slope form of the line. Then, we will simplify it into standard form.\n\nTo construct the point-slope form of the line, we need a point and a slope. We already have two points, so we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{6-5}{-1-(-5)}=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use this slope along with one of the points to write the point-slope form equation of the line. It doesn't matter which point we choose, but let's choose $(-5,5).$ The point-slope equation is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{1}{4}(x-(-5)) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo simplify the point-slope formula into standard form, we first multiply by $4$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-5 & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+5) \\\\ 4\\cdot (y-5) & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}(x+5) \\\\ 4y-20 & =x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we move the variables to the left side and the constant to the right side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y-20 & =x+5 \\\\ 4y & =x+25 \\\\ -x+4y & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply by $-1$ to make the $x$-coefficient positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+4y & =25 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-x+4y) & =(-1)\\cdot 25 \\\\ x-4y & =-25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the standard form of the equation is $x-4y=-25.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9a41d9d6-ad78-5b1c-ae8a-f0443259b92e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9a41d9d6-ad78-5b1c-ae8a-f0443259b92e" :answer-field/id #uuid "9a41d9d6-ad78-5b1c-ae8a-f0443259b92e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-efe8310a-c429-55cd-8c15-4311cc9072a1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b8f62edd-3019-5453-8498-1e1a4f6a7c08" "answer-bbbbf3ff-8039-54bc-bfab-f8b2a0aee979" "answer-37dc7839-3847-5f01-81ef-f713051f0840" "answer-864c8f78-6254-5494-8143-e61489b757d5" "answer-efe8310a-c429-55cd-8c15-4311cc9072a1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b8f62edd-3019-5453-8498-1e1a4f6a7c08" :answer/id #uuid "b8f62edd-3019-5453-8498-1e1a4f6a7c08" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2x-3y=-18" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bbbbf3ff-8039-54bc-bfab-f8b2a0aee979" :answer/id #uuid "bbbbf3ff-8039-54bc-bfab-f8b2a0aee979" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2x=3y-18" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-37dc7839-3847-5f01-81ef-f713051f0840" :answer/id #uuid "37dc7839-3847-5f01-81ef-f713051f0840" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5x+2y=8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-864c8f78-6254-5494-8143-e61489b757d5" :answer/id #uuid "864c8f78-6254-5494-8143-e61489b757d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x-4y+25=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-efe8310a-c429-55cd-8c15-4311cc9072a1" :answer/id #uuid "efe8310a-c429-55cd-8c15-4311cc9072a1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x-4y=-25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186088066 :question/problem "Express the equation of the line that passes through the points $(-3,-2)$ and $(-1,4)$ in standard form.\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we will construct the point-slope form of the line. Then, we will simplify it into standard form.\n\nTo construct the point-slope form of the line, we need a point and a slope. We already have two points, so we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{4-(-2)}{-1-(-3)}=\\frac{6}{2}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use this slope along with one of the points to write the point-slope form equation of the line. It doesn't matter which point we choose, but let's choose $(-1,4).$ The point-slope equation is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =3(x-(-1)) \\\\ y-4 & =3(x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we simplify the point-slope formula into standard form, moving the variables to the left side and the constant to the right side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-4 & =3(x+1) \\\\ y-4 & =3x+3 \\\\ y & =3x+7 \\\\ -3x+y & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply by $-1$ to make the $x$-coefficient positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x+y & =7 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-3x+y) & =(-1)\\cdot 7 \\\\ 3x-y & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the standard form of the equation is \n\n$$\n3x-y=-7.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186085127 :answer-field/correct "answer-ccfc196e-d2b9-5e51-b03e-0879a7618102" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ccfc196e-d2b9-5e51-b03e-0879a7618102"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ccfc196e-d2b9-5e51-b03e-0879a7618102" :answer/id #uuid "ccfc196e-d2b9-5e51-b03e-0879a7618102" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3x-y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186085126 :answer-field/correct "answer-592087b3-94fc-571b-8975-e20b1af01fdd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-592087b3-94fc-571b-8975-e20b1af01fdd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-592087b3-94fc-571b-8975-e20b1af01fdd" :answer/id #uuid "592087b3-94fc-571b-8975-e20b1af01fdd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186088991 :tutorial/content "The **standard form** equation of a line is \n\n$$\nax+by=c,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ are all integers,\n\n- $a \\geq 0,$ and\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ have no common factors.\n\nNote that we always write $x$ *before* $y$ on the left-hand side!\n\nLet's see some examples:\n\n- The equation $2x - y= -5$ is in standard form. Indeed, the variables are on the left-hand side, while the constant term is on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{2x - y}_{\\textrm{variables}} \\quad = \\underbrace{-5\\phantom{y}}_{\\textrm{constant}}\n$$\n\n \nAll coefficients are integers (namely, $2,$ $-1,$ and $-5$) and don't have common factors greater than $1.$ Also, the coefficient of $x$ is non-negative ($2$).\n\n- The equation $-3x + y = 1$ is *not* in standard form because the coefficient of $x$ is negative ($-3$).\n\n- The equation $2x+4y = -10$ is *not* in standard form because the coefficients $(2,4,$ and $-10)$ have a common factor of $2.$\n\n- The equation $\\dfrac12x+\\dfrac13y = 6$ is *not* in standard form because the coefficients of $x$ and $y$ are not integers." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186088993 :tutorial/content "Let's now discuss how to convert the equation of a line to standard form.\n\nTo illustrate, we will take the line $y = 2x-1$ and convert it into standard form. We begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x-1 \\\\ y-2x & =-1 \\\\ -2x+y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow the equation is in the form $ax+by=c.$ However, it violates the rule $a \\geq 0.$ We can fix this by multiplying the equation by $-1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+y & =-1 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-2x+y) & =(-1)\\cdot (-1) \\\\ 2x-y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a \\geq 0.$ So, we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is $2x-y=1.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087911 :activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "activity-58c37e26-62d4-5f02-baeb-ef4acb111c95" :activity/id #uuid "58c37e26-62d4-5f02-baeb-ef4acb111c95" :activity/title "Equations of Ellipses Centered at a General Point" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049699 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-7b5cb7cb-6b99-55c2-9630-9fedf41f6464" "step-5ebdf269-1a6a-5b92-a407-30fd48f96196" "step-4e77c8f2-3823-5cad-9a64-298eace8bbaf"] :activity/first-step "step-7b5cb7cb-6b99-55c2-9630-9fedf41f6464" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-7b5cb7cb-6b99-55c2-9630-9fedf41f6464" :step/id #uuid "7b5cb7cb-6b99-55c2-9630-9fedf41f6464" :step/content 17592186284551 :step/next "step-5ebdf269-1a6a-5b92-a407-30fd48f96196" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-5ebdf269-1a6a-5b92-a407-30fd48f96196" :step/id #uuid "5ebdf269-1a6a-5b92-a407-30fd48f96196" :step/content 17592186371583 :step/next "step-4e77c8f2-3823-5cad-9a64-298eace8bbaf" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-4e77c8f2-3823-5cad-9a64-298eace8bbaf" :step/id #uuid "4e77c8f2-3823-5cad-9a64-298eace8bbaf" :step/content 17592186250390 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186176479 :question/answer-fields 17592186175012] {:db/id 17592186176479 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2-2\\text{i} & 4 \\\\ -2 & -2-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(2-2\\text{i}){x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+(-2-2\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-2\\text{i}I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nOn the other hand, since ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the  complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}=\\overset{―}{{\\lambda }_{1}}=-2\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector of ${\\lambda }_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}=\\frac{(-2)(-2)}{(1-\\text{i})(1+\\text{i})}=2.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186326684 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=-2+3\\text{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2+3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1-3\\text{i} & 2 \\\\ -5 & 1-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(-1-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-2+3\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1-3\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, since ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+3\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the  complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}=\\overset{―}{{\\lambda }_{1}}=-2-3\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector to ${\\lambda }_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}=\\frac{(1-3\\text{i})(1+3\\text{i})}{(5)(5)}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186351199 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-2+\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2+\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3-\\text{i} & -2 \\\\ 5 & 3-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(-3-\\text{i}){x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+(3-\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-2+\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{5}(3-\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{5}(3-\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-5,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}3-\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-5}{3-\\text{i}}=\\frac{-5(3+\\text{i})}{10}=-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175929 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6b62f170-a433-5de2-8209-ac0435d5fb1d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-6b62f170-a433-5de2-8209-ac0435d5fb1d" :question/id #uuid "6b62f170-a433-5de2-8209-ac0435d5fb1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-81148" :question/problem "Determine whether $v=[\\begin{aligned}-2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4-8\\text{i} \\\\ 4-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4{\\text{i}}^{2}-8\\text{i} \\\\ 4-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2\\text{i}(4-2\\text{i}) \\\\ 1(4-2\\text{i})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(4-2\\text{i})[\\begin{aligned}-2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(4-2\\text{i})v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=(4-2\\text{i})v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =4-2\\text{i}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c0cfc4d0-a713-5f9b-8684-1ec443f7a134"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c0cfc4d0-a713-5f9b-8684-1ec443f7a134" :answer-field/id #uuid "c0cfc4d0-a713-5f9b-8684-1ec443f7a134" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7e689100-10cd-5e89-9858-aae9672b630a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-27933876-5b2f-59dc-a5a5-942ee704758c" "answer-9c3dfe99-6eeb-5edd-b06f-b616e7766a94" "answer-4695373b-5cc8-56e0-bcf9-e8857baa20e7" "answer-7e689100-10cd-5e89-9858-aae9672b630a" "answer-af381ec0-91d6-5997-9ce5-2412f090232d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-27933876-5b2f-59dc-a5a5-942ee704758c" :answer/id #uuid "27933876-5b2f-59dc-a5a5-942ee704758c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\lambda =2+\\text{i}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9c3dfe99-6eeb-5edd-b06f-b616e7766a94" :answer/id #uuid "9c3dfe99-6eeb-5edd-b06f-b616e7766a94" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\lambda =2-\\text{i}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4695373b-5cc8-56e0-bcf9-e8857baa20e7" :answer/id #uuid "4695373b-5cc8-56e0-bcf9-e8857baa20e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\lambda =4+2\\text{i}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e689100-10cd-5e89-9858-aae9672b630a" :answer/id #uuid "7e689100-10cd-5e89-9858-aae9672b630a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\lambda =4-2\\text{i}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-af381ec0-91d6-5997-9ce5-2412f090232d" :answer/id #uuid "af381ec0-91d6-5997-9ce5-2412f090232d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186351251 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =\\sqrt{5}\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i} & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}){x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+(-2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}(2+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}(2+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i} \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-3}{2+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}}=\\frac{-3(2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i})}{9}=-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\text{i}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "activity-aa14eb4a-61b9-5764-91b0-789a38c1720c" :activity/id #uuid "aa14eb4a-61b9-5764-91b0-789a38c1720c" :activity/title "Finding Complex Eigenvectors of Real 2x2 Matrices" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048973 :activity/course 17592186046659 :activity/steps ["step-64c77246-92ab-5343-a010-84041f103bc9" "step-9b37de30-2d0f-54d1-9978-97a780796cdb" "step-7bf20b01-76bc-543c-a0e8-b3bf2f4563c9" "step-7665ea0d-01a8-57f6-bbbc-8dc06d46bcb0" "step-156e434a-b38e-5003-b3b2-3990ebd3f421"] :activity/first-step "step-64c77246-92ab-5343-a010-84041f103bc9" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-64c77246-92ab-5343-a010-84041f103bc9" :step/id #uuid "64c77246-92ab-5343-a010-84041f103bc9" :step/content 17592186176479 :step/next "step-9b37de30-2d0f-54d1-9978-97a780796cdb" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-9b37de30-2d0f-54d1-9978-97a780796cdb" :step/id #uuid "9b37de30-2d0f-54d1-9978-97a780796cdb" :step/content 17592186326684 :step/next "step-7bf20b01-76bc-543c-a0e8-b3bf2f4563c9" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-7bf20b01-76bc-543c-a0e8-b3bf2f4563c9" :step/id #uuid "7bf20b01-76bc-543c-a0e8-b3bf2f4563c9" :step/content 17592186351199 :step/next "step-7665ea0d-01a8-57f6-bbbc-8dc06d46bcb0" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-7665ea0d-01a8-57f6-bbbc-8dc06d46bcb0" :step/id #uuid "7665ea0d-01a8-57f6-bbbc-8dc06d46bcb0" :step/content "question-6b62f170-a433-5de2-8209-ac0435d5fb1d" :step/next "step-156e434a-b38e-5003-b3b2-3990ebd3f421" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-156e434a-b38e-5003-b3b2-3990ebd3f421" :step/id #uuid "156e434a-b38e-5003-b3b2-3990ebd3f421" :step/content 17592186351251 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105743 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-151ea9a2-7fca-5107-a3ac-b15fd8b562e9"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186106471 :question/problem "${(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "To raise the fraction to the power of $2$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $2:$\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}=\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, using ${3}^{2}=9$ and ${5}^{2}=25,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}=\\frac{9}{25}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186104974 :answer-field/correct 17592186104977 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-151ea9a2-7fca-5107-a3ac-b15fd8b562e9" :question/id #uuid "151ea9a2-7fca-5107-a3ac-b15fd8b562e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-68461" :question/problem "${(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "To raise the fraction to the power of $3$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $3:$\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}=\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{2}^{3}}\n$$\n\nNow, using, ${1}^{3}=1$ and ${2}^{3}=8,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{2}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f8aa62db-1655-53a1-a21b-36bf03df3896"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f8aa62db-1655-53a1-a21b-36bf03df3896" :answer-field/id #uuid "f8aa62db-1655-53a1-a21b-36bf03df3896" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3fcd1bb2-1500-5ee2-b804-b199a4af59f8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f175e3a1-caba-5bec-9be2-4f528d2943b2" "answer-72f3cf98-f8a7-5839-b0ee-742e6ecc2ac4" "answer-36fd9457-00f5-537f-ad12-5edc30b6be51" "answer-3fcd1bb2-1500-5ee2-b804-b199a4af59f8" "answer-da258386-0ee6-57ce-b6d3-41ef50f8b2d6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f175e3a1-caba-5bec-9be2-4f528d2943b2" :answer/id #uuid "f175e3a1-caba-5bec-9be2-4f528d2943b2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-72f3cf98-f8a7-5839-b0ee-742e6ecc2ac4" :answer/id #uuid "72f3cf98-f8a7-5839-b0ee-742e6ecc2ac4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-36fd9457-00f5-537f-ad12-5edc30b6be51" :answer/id #uuid "36fd9457-00f5-537f-ad12-5edc30b6be51" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3fcd1bb2-1500-5ee2-b804-b199a4af59f8" :answer/id #uuid "3fcd1bb2-1500-5ee2-b804-b199a4af59f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-da258386-0ee6-57ce-b6d3-41ef50f8b2d6" :answer/id #uuid "da258386-0ee6-57ce-b6d3-41ef50f8b2d6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186106473 :question/problem "${(-\\frac{1}{7})}^{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $-\\frac{1}{7}=\\frac{(-1)}{7}.$\n\nTo raise the fraction to the power of $2$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $2:$\n\n$$\n{(-\\frac{1}{7})}^{2}={(\\frac{(-1)}{7})}^{2}=\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{{7}^{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, using $(-1)^{2}=1$ and ${7}^{2}=49,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{{7}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{49}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186104986 :answer-field/correct 17592186104988 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "activity-7e7b6dc7-4114-5b93-bb27-c0eeaa8b140f" :activity/id #uuid "7e7b6dc7-4114-5b93-bb27-c0eeaa8b140f" :activity/title "The Power of Quotient Rule for Exponents" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048683 :activity/course 17592186046648 :activity/steps ["step-9adbfcf0-8452-52d5-b4fb-e01320e32a18" "step-341ef616-a07f-5fa1-b233-8905f2e7e196" "step-b0856f94-0f86-5940-aa64-f087a7c6407e"] :activity/first-step "step-9adbfcf0-8452-52d5-b4fb-e01320e32a18" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-9adbfcf0-8452-52d5-b4fb-e01320e32a18" :step/id #uuid "9adbfcf0-8452-52d5-b4fb-e01320e32a18" :step/content 17592186106471 :step/next "step-341ef616-a07f-5fa1-b233-8905f2e7e196" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-341ef616-a07f-5fa1-b233-8905f2e7e196" :step/id #uuid "341ef616-a07f-5fa1-b233-8905f2e7e196" :step/content "question-151ea9a2-7fca-5107-a3ac-b15fd8b562e9" :step/next "step-b0856f94-0f86-5940-aa64-f087a7c6407e" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b0856f94-0f86-5940-aa64-f087a7c6407e" :step/id #uuid "b0856f94-0f86-5940-aa64-f087a7c6407e" :step/content 17592186106473 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125154 :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R$ and its subset $C=\\{[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,a\\in R\\}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $C$ is closed under matrix addition\n\nII. $C$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $C$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nIV. $C$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If ${A}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ and ${A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ are two matrices from $C,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a matrix from $C.$ So, $C$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $A=[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ is an element of $C$ and $k\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kA & =k[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}ka & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a matrix from $C.$ So, $C$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $C$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186123524 :answer-field/correct 17592186123528 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124488 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3ec301c4-26d6-5f2a-9132-b21928f5ee67"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-3ec301c4-26d6-5f2a-9132-b21928f5ee67" :question/id #uuid "3ec301c4-26d6-5f2a-9132-b21928f5ee67" :question/math-academy-id "q-94638" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $C[0,3]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,3]\\to R$ and its subset $N$ containing all functions that return a nonzero value at exactly one point in $[0,3].$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $N$ is closed under function addition\n\nII. $N$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $N$ is a subspace of $C[0,3]$\n\nIV. $N$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,3]$\n\n*Hint: Different functions from $N$ might give a nonzero value at different points in $[0,3].$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f({a}_{1})\\ne 0$ and $g({a}_{2})\\ne 0,$ where ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ are distinct points in $[0,3],$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({a}_{1})+g({a}_{1}) & \\ne 0 \\\\ f({a}_{2})+g({a}_{2}) & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)+g(x)\\ne 0$ at two distinct points. So, $N$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(a)\\ne 0$ for some $a\\in [0,3],$ and $k=0,$ then\n\n$$\nkf(x)=0\n$$\n\n \nfor any $x\\in [0,3].$ So, $N$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $N$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-28ce3e2a-2275-50a0-b25b-b9f3436f743c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-28ce3e2a-2275-50a0-b25b-b9f3436f743c" :answer-field/id #uuid "28ce3e2a-2275-50a0-b25b-b9f3436f743c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-25ce7efb-3ad5-579b-809f-0dd2e31fe7e7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-aca0bca7-0e7a-58b7-a3c4-843dab2110e5" "answer-be1fae9a-76f7-5ae7-af20-eb786be794c4" "answer-87f00bc1-a093-5b90-a9c9-83e2777d88d8" "answer-f14395c6-36b4-5cb6-8085-7a9935a781a6" "answer-25ce7efb-3ad5-579b-809f-0dd2e31fe7e7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aca0bca7-0e7a-58b7-a3c4-843dab2110e5" :answer/id #uuid "aca0bca7-0e7a-58b7-a3c4-843dab2110e5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-be1fae9a-76f7-5ae7-af20-eb786be794c4" :answer/id #uuid "be1fae9a-76f7-5ae7-af20-eb786be794c4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-87f00bc1-a093-5b90-a9c9-83e2777d88d8" :answer/id #uuid "87f00bc1-a093-5b90-a9c9-83e2777d88d8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f14395c6-36b4-5cb6-8085-7a9935a781a6" :answer/id #uuid "f14395c6-36b4-5cb6-8085-7a9935a781a6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-25ce7efb-3ad5-579b-809f-0dd2e31fe7e7" :answer/id #uuid "25ce7efb-3ad5-579b-809f-0dd2e31fe7e7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-bba4c4cc-2793-5afd-91e2-7a137ae53cb5" :activity/id #uuid "bba4c4cc-2793-5afd-91e2-7a137ae53cb5" :activity/title "Subspaces of Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050453 :activity/course 17592186046659 :activity/steps ["step-87d932f6-49ba-5c24-8d19-a6ad3580ecf9" "step-876842ab-2531-5a55-8715-683cc46f3d77" "step-61b43f65-40c4-5ee2-a729-b625837e3d66" "step-ef5989bf-617a-54c7-bbc7-9e531c1f1c5b"] :activity/first-step "step-87d932f6-49ba-5c24-8d19-a6ad3580ecf9" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-87d932f6-49ba-5c24-8d19-a6ad3580ecf9" :step/id #uuid "87d932f6-49ba-5c24-8d19-a6ad3580ecf9" :step/content 17592186125154 :step/next "step-876842ab-2531-5a55-8715-683cc46f3d77" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-876842ab-2531-5a55-8715-683cc46f3d77" :step/id #uuid "876842ab-2531-5a55-8715-683cc46f3d77" :step/content 17592186329727 :step/next "step-61b43f65-40c4-5ee2-a729-b625837e3d66" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-61b43f65-40c4-5ee2-a729-b625837e3d66" :step/id #uuid "61b43f65-40c4-5ee2-a729-b625837e3d66" :step/content "question-3ec301c4-26d6-5f2a-9132-b21928f5ee67" :step/next "step-ef5989bf-617a-54c7-bbc7-9e531c1f1c5b" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-ef5989bf-617a-54c7-bbc7-9e531c1f1c5b" :step/id #uuid "ef5989bf-617a-54c7-bbc7-9e531c1f1c5b" :step/content 17592186360468 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186268559 :answer-field/choices 17592186267444] [:db/retract 17592186268559 :answer-field/choices 17592186268294] [:db/retract 17592186268559 :answer-field/choices 17592186266263] [:db/retract 17592186268559 :answer-field/choices 17592186267786] [:db/retract 17592186268559 :answer-field/choices 17592186266159] {:db/id 17592186124466 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-970e7795-a408-5210-80aa-25f375509b0b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-970e7795-a408-5210-80aa-25f375509b0b" :question/id #uuid "970e7795-a408-5210-80aa-25f375509b0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-65263" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y,z)={e}^{\\sqrt{xyz}}?$\n\nI. The partial derivative with respect to $x$ is defined at $(1,-1,-1)$\n\nII. ${f}_{y}(-1,1,1)$ is defined\n\nIII. ${f}_{z}(1,1,0)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & ={e}^{\\sqrt{xyz}}\\cdot \\frac{yz}{2\\sqrt{xyz}} \\\\  & =\\frac{yz{e}^{\\sqrt{xyz}}}{2\\sqrt{xyz}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & ={e}^{\\sqrt{xyz}}\\cdot \\frac{xz}{2\\sqrt{xyz}} \\\\  & =\\frac{xz{e}^{\\sqrt{xyz}}}{2\\sqrt{xyz}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & ={e}^{\\sqrt{xyz}}\\cdot \\frac{xy}{2\\sqrt{xyz}} \\\\  & =\\frac{xy{e}^{\\sqrt{xyz}}}{2\\sqrt{xyz}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,-1,-1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(1,-1,-1)={e}^{\\sqrt{(1)(-1)(-1)}}={e}^{\\sqrt{1}}=e,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{x}$ is also defined at $(1,-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,-1,-1) & =\\frac{(-1)(-1){e}^{\\sqrt{(1)(-1)(-1)}}}{2\\sqrt{(1)(-1)(-1)}}=\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{1}}}{2\\sqrt{1}}=\\frac{e}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(-1,1,1)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(-1,1,1)={e}^{\\sqrt{(-1)(1)(1)}}={e}^{\\sqrt{-1}},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have a negative number under the radical. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement III is false. If we substitute $(1,1,0)$ into ${f}_{z}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{z}(1,1,0)=\\frac{1\\cdot 1\\cdot {e}^{\\sqrt{1\\cdot 1\\cdot 0}}}{2\\sqrt{1\\cdot 1\\cdot 0}}=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I  only'." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8c27c81b-25fa-5185-a05b-37e0f78454cf"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8c27c81b-25fa-5185-a05b-37e0f78454cf" :answer-field/id #uuid "8c27c81b-25fa-5185-a05b-37e0f78454cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6a0ef88e-c7ac-5cdc-ae0e-1efc196b01b2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d3bc4946-5ae0-59ab-8397-c509f69337df" "answer-6a0ef88e-c7ac-5cdc-ae0e-1efc196b01b2" "answer-084ca7fb-683b-515d-bd79-6b47f18630b7" "answer-1352f5a0-9197-5682-a147-c4c731774502" "answer-82a72c3b-2c62-5d09-a02a-39b20b9aa673"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d3bc4946-5ae0-59ab-8397-c509f69337df" :answer/id #uuid "d3bc4946-5ae0-59ab-8397-c509f69337df" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6a0ef88e-c7ac-5cdc-ae0e-1efc196b01b2" :answer/id #uuid "6a0ef88e-c7ac-5cdc-ae0e-1efc196b01b2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-084ca7fb-683b-515d-bd79-6b47f18630b7" :answer/id #uuid "084ca7fb-683b-515d-bd79-6b47f18630b7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1352f5a0-9197-5682-a147-c4c731774502" :answer/id #uuid "1352f5a0-9197-5682-a147-c4c731774502" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-82a72c3b-2c62-5d09-a02a-39b20b9aa673" :answer/id #uuid "82a72c3b-2c62-5d09-a02a-39b20b9aa673" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186268903 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $g(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)}{z}$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1>0$ and $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of $g$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{g}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1,\\,z\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${g}_{x}(x,y,z)=\\frac{2x}{z({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)}$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1\\ne 0$ and $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{x}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 1,\\,z\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domain of ${g}_{y}(x,y,z)$ is the same: \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{y}}={D}_{{g}_{x}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 1,\\,z\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${g}_{z}(x,y,z)=-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)}{{z}^{2}}$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1>0$ and $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{z}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{z}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1,\\,z\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $g$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{g}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{z}} & ={D}_{g}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{z}} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1,\\,z\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 1,\\,z\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1,\\,z\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1,\\,z\\ne 0\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186268559 :answer-field/correct "answer-2ae2e06a-4a60-5d0a-89f7-a1206ad8886c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ee12a67f-64f9-5a94-956a-3d8cefbc2906" "answer-2ae2e06a-4a60-5d0a-89f7-a1206ad8886c" "answer-1e9af209-8790-5051-b760-8a2b7ab58c1e" "answer-f1099e4d-5739-5e2b-abdc-97a47dbd0d32" "answer-4da31340-f280-5ea2-b625-950af86420b9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ee12a67f-64f9-5a94-956a-3d8cefbc2906" :answer/id #uuid "ee12a67f-64f9-5a94-956a-3d8cefbc2906" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0,\\,z>0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2ae2e06a-4a60-5d0a-89f7-a1206ad8886c" :answer/id #uuid "2ae2e06a-4a60-5d0a-89f7-a1206ad8886c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1,\\,z\\ne 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1e9af209-8790-5051-b760-8a2b7ab58c1e" :answer/id #uuid "1e9af209-8790-5051-b760-8a2b7ab58c1e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f1099e4d-5739-5e2b-abdc-97a47dbd0d32" :answer/id #uuid "f1099e4d-5739-5e2b-abdc-97a47dbd0d32" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 1,\\,z\\ne 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4da31340-f280-5ea2-b625-950af86420b9" :answer/id #uuid "4da31340-f280-5ea2-b625-950af86420b9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-345ef93a-3614-5fac-b24e-50dfc8791798" :activity/id #uuid "345ef93a-3614-5fac-b24e-50dfc8791798" :activity/title "Partial Differentiability of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048877 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-f230ec9a-d682-55d8-adbb-af178ec09394" "step-7f1dcee5-fe50-54b8-9541-d95743760a08" "step-87fc833d-38e2-5311-a2c8-c3e5fcb40ab1" "step-79e002bf-37b0-5c5a-a563-f450eae54650" "step-60152d46-a564-5e18-b836-e8be243798bf"] :activity/first-step "step-f230ec9a-d682-55d8-adbb-af178ec09394" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-f230ec9a-d682-55d8-adbb-af178ec09394" :step/id #uuid "f230ec9a-d682-55d8-adbb-af178ec09394" :step/content 17592186124995 :step/next "step-7f1dcee5-fe50-54b8-9541-d95743760a08" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-7f1dcee5-fe50-54b8-9541-d95743760a08" :step/id #uuid "7f1dcee5-fe50-54b8-9541-d95743760a08" :step/content "question-970e7795-a408-5210-80aa-25f375509b0b" :step/next "step-87fc833d-38e2-5311-a2c8-c3e5fcb40ab1" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-87fc833d-38e2-5311-a2c8-c3e5fcb40ab1" :step/id #uuid "87fc833d-38e2-5311-a2c8-c3e5fcb40ab1" :step/content 17592186268903 :step/next "step-79e002bf-37b0-5c5a-a563-f450eae54650" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-79e002bf-37b0-5c5a-a563-f450eae54650" :step/id #uuid "79e002bf-37b0-5c5a-a563-f450eae54650" :step/content 17592186426720 :step/next "step-60152d46-a564-5e18-b836-e8be243798bf" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-60152d46-a564-5e18-b836-e8be243798bf" :step/id #uuid "60152d46-a564-5e18-b836-e8be243798bf" :step/content 17592186268906 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152300 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4227b37a-8e18-5afd-83bf-26143e32cd4d" "question-d4dc1b86-0478-57c7-a48b-3ee42621256c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-4227b37a-8e18-5afd-83bf-26143e32cd4d" :question/id #uuid "4227b37a-8e18-5afd-83bf-26143e32cd4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-68842" :question/problem "$2÷\\frac{2}{5}\\times 4=$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nWe divide and multiply, from left to right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2÷\\frac{2}{5}\\times 4 & = \\\\ (2÷\\frac{2}{5})\\times 4 & = \\\\ (2\\times \\frac{5}{2})\\times 4 & = \\\\ (\\frac{2}{1}\\times \\frac{5}{2})\\times 4 & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\times 5}{1\\times 2}\\times 4 & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}\\times 5}{1\\times \\cancel{2}}\\times 4 & = \\\\ 5\\times 4 & = \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ae7bcc7d-7b8b-541d-a96a-a62bcd62870a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ae7bcc7d-7b8b-541d-a96a-a62bcd62870a" :answer-field/id #uuid "ae7bcc7d-7b8b-541d-a96a-a62bcd62870a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-12992113-2342-5279-9f39-93240a16d420" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9f727f0d-4e72-5891-b43a-44489bd73c19" "answer-12992113-2342-5279-9f39-93240a16d420" "answer-96d4b2d0-aff1-5142-9b3d-ae023cf3bdf8" "answer-b594613f-b609-5d9f-b507-7ed04d8bb283" "answer-e4eed5e2-fde8-5761-9397-e3a699b2d564"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9f727f0d-4e72-5891-b43a-44489bd73c19" :answer/id #uuid "9f727f0d-4e72-5891-b43a-44489bd73c19" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-12992113-2342-5279-9f39-93240a16d420" :answer/id #uuid "12992113-2342-5279-9f39-93240a16d420" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-96d4b2d0-aff1-5142-9b3d-ae023cf3bdf8" :answer/id #uuid "96d4b2d0-aff1-5142-9b3d-ae023cf3bdf8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "30" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b594613f-b609-5d9f-b507-7ed04d8bb283" :answer/id #uuid "b594613f-b609-5d9f-b507-7ed04d8bb283" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "40" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4eed5e2-fde8-5761-9397-e3a699b2d564" :answer/id #uuid "e4eed5e2-fde8-5761-9397-e3a699b2d564" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152302 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c9b4fb98-fcb9-51e9-9d1c-a0e33aad2c53"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-c9b4fb98-fcb9-51e9-9d1c-a0e33aad2c53" :question/id #uuid "c9b4fb98-fcb9-51e9-9d1c-a0e33aad2c53" :question/math-academy-id "q-175320" :question/problem "$(-4)\\times (2.3-0.1)=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nFirst, we perform the operation inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)\\times (2.3-0.1) & = \\\\ (-4)\\times 2.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)\\times 2.2 & =-8.8\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c4974698-7ea2-58ec-9b02-4e54bbb36aee"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c4974698-7ea2-58ec-9b02-4e54bbb36aee" :answer-field/id #uuid "c4974698-7ea2-58ec-9b02-4e54bbb36aee" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-3c7ca818-f28b-5d60-b216-a2e87b1e5db2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3c7ca818-f28b-5d60-b216-a2e87b1e5db2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c7ca818-f28b-5d60-b216-a2e87b1e5db2" :answer/id #uuid "3c7ca818-f28b-5d60-b216-a2e87b1e5db2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8.8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d4dc1b86-0478-57c7-a48b-3ee42621256c" :question/id #uuid "d4dc1b86-0478-57c7-a48b-3ee42621256c" :question/math-academy-id "q-175957" :question/problem "Expressed as an integer, $(-4)÷(\\frac{2}{5})\\times 6=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nWe multiply and divide, from left to right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)÷\\frac{2}{5}\\times 6 & = \\\\ ((-4)÷\\frac{2}{5})\\times 6 & = \\\\ (\\frac{-4}{1}\\times \\frac{5}{2})\\times 6 & = \\\\ (\\frac{(-4)\\times 5}{1\\times 2})\\times 6 & = \\\\ (-\\frac{20}{2})\\times 6 & = \\\\ (-10)\\times 6 & = \\\\ -60\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5932016c-ec0a-5d79-9f39-b11ee0e695a8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5932016c-ec0a-5d79-9f39-b11ee0e695a8" :answer-field/id #uuid "5932016c-ec0a-5d79-9f39-b11ee0e695a8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-6da77181-6032-5e5c-929d-4a017fc0454f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6da77181-6032-5e5c-929d-4a017fc0454f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6da77181-6032-5e5c-929d-4a017fc0454f" :answer/id #uuid "6da77181-6032-5e5c-929d-4a017fc0454f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-60" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-93d82c70-ff29-55ed-9d22-40031a1c626d" :activity/id #uuid "93d82c70-ff29-55ed-9d22-40031a1c626d" :activity/title "Evaluating Rational Number Expressions Without Exponents" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048888 :activity/course 17592186046648 :activity/steps ["step-8bcf62cd-f6d0-59f6-b33f-9300365fc7dc" "step-13d18f39-8c3e-50cf-926a-da6be8b2ee39" "step-7f0b0168-fedc-5b19-9f11-bf9a623d5ec4"] :activity/first-step "step-8bcf62cd-f6d0-59f6-b33f-9300365fc7dc" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-8bcf62cd-f6d0-59f6-b33f-9300365fc7dc" :step/id #uuid "8bcf62cd-f6d0-59f6-b33f-9300365fc7dc" :step/content "question-4227b37a-8e18-5afd-83bf-26143e32cd4d" :step/next "step-13d18f39-8c3e-50cf-926a-da6be8b2ee39" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-13d18f39-8c3e-50cf-926a-da6be8b2ee39" :step/id #uuid "13d18f39-8c3e-50cf-926a-da6be8b2ee39" :step/content "question-c9b4fb98-fcb9-51e9-9d1c-a0e33aad2c53" :step/next "step-7f0b0168-fedc-5b19-9f11-bf9a623d5ec4" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-7f0b0168-fedc-5b19-9f11-bf9a623d5ec4" :step/id #uuid "7f0b0168-fedc-5b19-9f11-bf9a623d5ec4" :step/content "question-d4dc1b86-0478-57c7-a48b-3ee42621256c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192636 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ddcdce51-4bc7-55a4-81e7-a74a7a6b3b12" "question-baeaa0a7-bfc7-5111-9e74-1fdaefb89ea5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-ddcdce51-4bc7-55a4-81e7-a74a7a6b3b12" :question/id #uuid "ddcdce51-4bc7-55a4-81e7-a74a7a6b3b12" :question/math-academy-id "q-112321" :question/problem "If $7|2x-5|+1=15,$ then $x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the absolute value on the left side of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7|2x-5|+1 & =15 \\\\ 7|2x-5| & =14 \\\\ |2x-5| & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following two cases:\n\n- If $|2x-5|=2x-5,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2x-5| & =2 \\\\ 2x-5 & =2 \\\\ 2x & =7 \\\\ x & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $|2x-5|=-(2x-5),$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2x-5| & =2 \\\\ -(2x-5) & =2 \\\\ -2x+5 & =2 \\\\ -2x & =-3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $x=\\frac{7}{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4c98fed7-20db-50af-9e49-c1f0c73826db"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4c98fed7-20db-50af-9e49-c1f0c73826db" :answer-field/id #uuid "4c98fed7-20db-50af-9e49-c1f0c73826db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-aff73d6c-ddcc-5140-9177-17ad84cce051" :answer-field/choices ["answer-affdc6a4-65f8-5318-8a1f-de4178ca2934" "answer-bb371206-32f6-5ce9-b43b-759d66c0fc02" "answer-1b21a6eb-0eb6-506f-bd93-3ef9b95b88e2" "answer-cd145d96-8415-53a5-bbc1-52861eca7892" "answer-aff73d6c-ddcc-5140-9177-17ad84cce051"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-affdc6a4-65f8-5318-8a1f-de4178ca2934" :answer/id #uuid "affdc6a4-65f8-5318-8a1f-de4178ca2934" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{7}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bb371206-32f6-5ce9-b43b-759d66c0fc02" :answer/id #uuid "bb371206-32f6-5ce9-b43b-759d66c0fc02" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-\\frac{7}{2},\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1b21a6eb-0eb6-506f-bd93-3ef9b95b88e2" :answer/id #uuid "1b21a6eb-0eb6-506f-bd93-3ef9b95b88e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=3,\\frac{7}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd145d96-8415-53a5-bbc1-52861eca7892" :answer/id #uuid "cd145d96-8415-53a5-bbc1-52861eca7892" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\frac{3}{2},7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aff73d6c-ddcc-5140-9177-17ad84cce051" :answer/id #uuid "aff73d6c-ddcc-5140-9177-17ad84cce051" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{7}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-baeaa0a7-bfc7-5111-9e74-1fdaefb89ea5" :question/id #uuid "baeaa0a7-bfc7-5111-9e74-1fdaefb89ea5" :question/math-academy-id "q-153975" :question/problem "If $3|2x-5|+13=4,$ then $x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the absolute value on the left side of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3|2x-5|+13 & =4 \\\\ 3|2x-5| & =-9 \\\\ |2x-5| & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the right-hand side is negative. Therefore, the equation has no solutions." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a1189cdb-d12a-5c98-a111-701599799875"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a1189cdb-d12a-5c98-a111-701599799875" :answer-field/id #uuid "a1189cdb-d12a-5c98-a111-701599799875" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-339b4405-4f1c-5518-8af3-9771ff47c479" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1a8ff519-a9c2-54ab-887f-1979423b6e33" "answer-b9568ea7-ed57-5f55-98df-37b6253df3dc" "answer-783c039e-76a4-5d62-ae61-0594245c1c1c" "answer-0a298de8-fcdb-50a2-a4f1-108440d7dce0" "answer-339b4405-4f1c-5518-8af3-9771ff47c479"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1a8ff519-a9c2-54ab-887f-1979423b6e33" :answer/id #uuid "1a8ff519-a9c2-54ab-887f-1979423b6e33" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-1,4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b9568ea7-ed57-5f55-98df-37b6253df3dc" :answer/id #uuid "b9568ea7-ed57-5f55-98df-37b6253df3dc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-4,-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-783c039e-76a4-5d62-ae61-0594245c1c1c" :answer/id #uuid "783c039e-76a4-5d62-ae61-0594245c1c1c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-4,1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0a298de8-fcdb-50a2-a4f1-108440d7dce0" :answer/id #uuid "0a298de8-fcdb-50a2-a4f1-108440d7dce0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=1,4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-339b4405-4f1c-5518-8af3-9771ff47c479" :answer/id #uuid "339b4405-4f1c-5518-8af3-9771ff47c479" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "No solutions exist" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192635 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2e7c0ce7-7ffd-571f-9543-de7ea37af680"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-2e7c0ce7-7ffd-571f-9543-de7ea37af680" :question/id #uuid "2e7c0ce7-7ffd-571f-9543-de7ea37af680" :question/math-academy-id "q-154664" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has *no* solution?\n\nI. $|5z+1|=5$\n\nII. $|5z+1|-3=-1$\n\nIII. $|5z+1|=-6$" :question/worked-solution "An absolute value equation has no solution if, when the absolute value is isolated on the left side, the right-hand side is negative.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each equation in turn:\n\n- Equation I has a solution. The absolute value is already isolated on the left-hand side, and the right-hand side is non-negative.\n\n- Equation II has a solution. To see this, we first isolate the absolute value on the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|5z+1|-3 & =-1 \\\\ |5z+1| & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the right-hand side is non-negative, this equation has a solution.\n\n- Equation III *has no solution*. The absolute value is already isolated on the left-hand side, and the right-hand side is *negative*.\n\nTherefore, the answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-09294ff2-d9d6-52b7-9077-c43a5e430b15"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-09294ff2-d9d6-52b7-9077-c43a5e430b15" :answer-field/id #uuid "09294ff2-d9d6-52b7-9077-c43a5e430b15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-dfbb0dd3-b661-558a-85dd-839040af8d8b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-74f22dec-1b1b-5ef1-9c84-1d364998af3d" "answer-3a80c8ae-ff2e-5357-9292-0da8b013d1ac" "answer-7a8b64d5-61ee-523a-aa6a-0686a98ce1a6" "answer-a785c005-45dd-5940-8127-a83f51ea5976" "answer-dfbb0dd3-b661-558a-85dd-839040af8d8b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-74f22dec-1b1b-5ef1-9c84-1d364998af3d" :answer/id #uuid "74f22dec-1b1b-5ef1-9c84-1d364998af3d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a80c8ae-ff2e-5357-9292-0da8b013d1ac" :answer/id #uuid "3a80c8ae-ff2e-5357-9292-0da8b013d1ac" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7a8b64d5-61ee-523a-aa6a-0686a98ce1a6" :answer/id #uuid "7a8b64d5-61ee-523a-aa6a-0686a98ce1a6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a785c005-45dd-5940-8127-a83f51ea5976" :answer/id #uuid "a785c005-45dd-5940-8127-a83f51ea5976" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dfbb0dd3-b661-558a-85dd-839040af8d8b" :answer/id #uuid "dfbb0dd3-b661-558a-85dd-839040af8d8b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-f892b1c0-ec68-5601-bffd-cb6018c52093" :activity/id #uuid "f892b1c0-ec68-5601-bffd-cb6018c52093" :activity/title "Further Absolute Value Equations" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050448 :activity/course 17592186046648 :activity/steps ["step-d8e7db10-2590-5657-8dfe-a36aa1597a4e" "step-57cb19e7-4470-5b79-801f-a29347b19b6a" "step-e0b2caf9-5a3f-5708-9cbd-b6253216f8e1"] :activity/first-step "step-d8e7db10-2590-5657-8dfe-a36aa1597a4e" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-d8e7db10-2590-5657-8dfe-a36aa1597a4e" :step/id #uuid "d8e7db10-2590-5657-8dfe-a36aa1597a4e" :step/content "question-ddcdce51-4bc7-55a4-81e7-a74a7a6b3b12" :step/next "step-57cb19e7-4470-5b79-801f-a29347b19b6a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-57cb19e7-4470-5b79-801f-a29347b19b6a" :step/id #uuid "57cb19e7-4470-5b79-801f-a29347b19b6a" :step/content "question-baeaa0a7-bfc7-5111-9e74-1fdaefb89ea5" :step/next "step-e0b2caf9-5a3f-5708-9cbd-b6253216f8e1" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e0b2caf9-5a3f-5708-9cbd-b6253216f8e1" :step/id #uuid "e0b2caf9-5a3f-5708-9cbd-b6253216f8e1" :step/content "question-2e7c0ce7-7ffd-571f-9543-de7ea37af680" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186127057 :answer-field/choices 17592186127058] [:db/retract 17592186127057 :answer-field/choices 17592186127059] [:db/retract 17592186127057 :answer-field/choices 17592186127060] [:db/retract 17592186127057 :answer-field/choices 17592186127061] [:db/retract 17592186127057 :answer-field/choices 17592186127062] {:db/id 17592186129079 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a1c45efd-b454-595e-a933-aae318d15f95"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129596 :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y,z)=x{e}^{y}-z$, find the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,1,e).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent plane to the level surface $f(x,y,z)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})=(1,1,e):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{y}-z)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{y}-z)\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{e}^{y}-z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & ={e}^{y}\\text{ }i+x{e}^{y}\\text{ }j-k \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,1,e) & ={e}^{1}\\text{ }i+(1){e}^{1}\\text{ }j-k \\\\  & =e\\,i+e\\,j-k \\\\  & =⟨e,e,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(1,1,e)\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-1,\\,z-e⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨e,e,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-1,\\,z-e⟩ & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186127057 :answer-field/correct "answer-4d8e49ba-c572-585b-9000-22f731c9e31c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7fd70068-b6fc-58b5-a8fd-0b6d0f6cdc1d" "answer-13a034a0-40db-53e2-8743-0500f64927fd" "answer-a4259989-16f4-5162-93ea-522cff1adbaa" "answer-b48376c2-fa7d-5ea7-a3f5-9c2f64a3454e" "answer-4d8e49ba-c572-585b-9000-22f731c9e31c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7fd70068-b6fc-58b5-a8fd-0b6d0f6cdc1d" :answer/id #uuid "7fd70068-b6fc-58b5-a8fd-0b6d0f6cdc1d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨1,1,-e⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-1,\\,z-e⟩=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-13a034a0-40db-53e2-8743-0500f64927fd" :answer/id #uuid "13a034a0-40db-53e2-8743-0500f64927fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨1,1,-e⟩\\cdot ⟨x-e,\\,y-e,\\,z-1⟩=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a4259989-16f4-5162-93ea-522cff1adbaa" :answer/id #uuid "a4259989-16f4-5162-93ea-522cff1adbaa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨1,1,e⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-1,\\,z-e⟩=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b48376c2-fa7d-5ea7-a3f5-9c2f64a3454e" :answer/id #uuid "b48376c2-fa7d-5ea7-a3f5-9c2f64a3454e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨e,e,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y+1,\\,z+e⟩=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4d8e49ba-c572-585b-9000-22f731c9e31c" :answer/id #uuid "4d8e49ba-c572-585b-9000-22f731c9e31c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨e,e,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-1,\\,z-e⟩=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a1c45efd-b454-595e-a933-aae318d15f95" :question/id #uuid "a1c45efd-b454-595e-a933-aae318d15f95" :question/math-academy-id "q-102700" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y,z)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-y{e}^{z}$, find the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent plane to the level surface $f(x,y,z)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})=(1,2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-y{e}^{z})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-y{e}^{z})\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-y{e}^{z}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x}\\text{ }i-{e}^{z}\\text{ }j-y{e}^{z}k \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,2,1) & =\\frac{2}{1}\\text{ }i-{e}^{1}\\text{ }j-(2){e}^{1}k \\\\  & =2\\,i-e\\,j-2ek \\\\  & =⟨2,-e,-2e⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(1,2,1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2,\\,z-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨2,-e,-2e⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2,\\,z-1⟩ & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-49ccc9b7-9d2d-5fab-9305-0a2ddb6c4989"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-49ccc9b7-9d2d-5fab-9305-0a2ddb6c4989" :answer-field/id #uuid "49ccc9b7-9d2d-5fab-9305-0a2ddb6c4989" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3050b9e4-da08-5715-ad5a-8caf10a32624" :answer-field/choices ["answer-67d37bdf-2be9-5b4b-b6fd-993f6357958d" "answer-3050b9e4-da08-5715-ad5a-8caf10a32624" "answer-a09c4894-0619-505a-853a-743d133d9d86" "answer-2c75f8c9-6d90-5e2f-9fd0-6d4f8cbd173a" "answer-472c72f7-428a-5c92-9b64-36649f199a43"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-67d37bdf-2be9-5b4b-b6fd-993f6357958d" :answer/id #uuid "67d37bdf-2be9-5b4b-b6fd-993f6357958d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨1,e,-2e⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2,\\,z-1⟩=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3050b9e4-da08-5715-ad5a-8caf10a32624" :answer/id #uuid "3050b9e4-da08-5715-ad5a-8caf10a32624" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨2,-e,-2e⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2,\\,z-1⟩=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a09c4894-0619-505a-853a-743d133d9d86" :answer/id #uuid "a09c4894-0619-505a-853a-743d133d9d86" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨2,e,-e⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y+2,\\,z+1⟩=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2c75f8c9-6d90-5e2f-9fd0-6d4f8cbd173a" :answer/id #uuid "2c75f8c9-6d90-5e2f-9fd0-6d4f8cbd173a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨2,e,2e⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y+2,\\,z+1⟩=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-472c72f7-428a-5c92-9b64-36649f199a43" :answer/id #uuid "472c72f7-428a-5c92-9b64-36649f199a43" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨e,1,2e⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2,\\,z-1⟩=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-63e75d2f-a87d-5228-8980-4d6f3fc9a47a" :activity/id #uuid "63e75d2f-a87d-5228-8980-4d6f3fc9a47a" :activity/title "Tangent Planes to Level Surfaces" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048554 :activity/course 17592186046659 :activity/steps ["step-f7e115a4-0109-5dcd-be67-0eff6d8c34d2" "step-b8b241c5-9ed0-5065-b6d8-8a3dd561fd54" "step-7ecf0ade-c143-56c2-83e7-b60005d8d75d" "step-867c6d88-e80e-5619-af3d-96e820e79d18" "step-8542cb45-fe3e-585c-beae-8f3803d3fd88"] :activity/first-step "step-f7e115a4-0109-5dcd-be67-0eff6d8c34d2" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-f7e115a4-0109-5dcd-be67-0eff6d8c34d