e power of quotient rule and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{s}{2})}^{4}{(\\frac{4}{t})}^{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{{s}^{4}}{{2}^{4}}\\cdot \\frac{{4}^{2}}{{t}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{{s}^{4}}{16}\\cdot \\frac{16}{{t}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{16{s}^{4}}{16{t}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{{s}^{4}}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b1dcbc3c-c562-52fc-969a-fd133bdc4cdc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b1dcbc3c-c562-52fc-969a-fd133bdc4cdc" :answer-field/id #uuid "b1dcbc3c-c562-52fc-969a-fd133bdc4cdc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d19d7351-db8e-5855-b293-25857ae699e2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-05f097c2-d349-566d-b1a7-de1e08a5e932" "answer-81552270-b895-5d9c-aa3e-247dcc5c61c8" "answer-5835df03-1013-5074-bc17-921ef4066c19" "answer-77d79dfc-932a-53e0-932b-d5746983cdec" "answer-d19d7351-db8e-5855-b293-25857ae699e2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-05f097c2-d349-566d-b1a7-de1e08a5e932" :answer/id #uuid "05f097c2-d349-566d-b1a7-de1e08a5e932" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2{s}^{2}}{{t}^{4}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-81552270-b895-5d9c-aa3e-247dcc5c61c8" :answer/id #uuid "81552270-b895-5d9c-aa3e-247dcc5c61c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{8{s}^{4}}{{t}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5835df03-1013-5074-bc17-921ef4066c19" :answer/id #uuid "5835df03-1013-5074-bc17-921ef4066c19" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{s}^{2}}{2{t}^{4}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-77d79dfc-932a-53e0-932b-d5746983cdec" :answer/id #uuid "77d79dfc-932a-53e0-932b-d5746983cdec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{s}^{4}}{4{t}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d19d7351-db8e-5855-b293-25857ae699e2" :answer/id #uuid "d19d7351-db8e-5855-b293-25857ae699e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{s}^{4}}{{t}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2b75a1b6-f44a-5a5a-8342-d11fe3f5f88d" :question/id #uuid "2b75a1b6-f44a-5a5a-8342-d11fe3f5f88d" :question/math-academy-id "q-72638" :question/problem "${(\\frac{3}{4x})}^{2}{(\\frac{-2x}{5})}^{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the power of quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{3}{4x})}^{2}{(\\frac{-2x}{5})}^{3}=\\frac{{3}^{2}}{(4x)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{(-2x)^{3}}{{5}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the power of product rule and multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{3}^{2}}{(4x)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{(-2x)^{3}}{{5}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{{3}^{2}}{{4}^{2}{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{(-2)^{3}{x}^{3}}{{5}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{9}{16{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{-8{x}^{3}}{125} & = \\\\ \\frac{-8\\cdot 9{x}^{3}}{16\\cdot 125{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the quotient rule and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-8\\cdot 9{x}^{3}}{16\\cdot 125{x}^{2}} & = \\\\ -\\frac{9{x}^{3-2}}{2\\cdot 125} & = \\\\ -\\frac{9x}{250}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-55b1ecfe-bba9-59e4-a0d9-5e7720e663f5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-55b1ecfe-bba9-59e4-a0d9-5e7720e663f5" :answer-field/id #uuid "55b1ecfe-bba9-59e4-a0d9-5e7720e663f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7fee2a5e-fafd-50c8-b51c-2713c379fc2f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4fe68738-1add-5c51-b2f9-83c9eca8db5b" "answer-ad881ae7-fad5-5cfc-afce-bd920e92937c" "answer-7fee2a5e-fafd-50c8-b51c-2713c379fc2f" "answer-0b428a97-41e2-590a-aace-24167e2b3de2" "answer-f31e6fee-19f2-52ae-9c5f-033e7ed7a3be"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4fe68738-1add-5c51-b2f9-83c9eca8db5b" :answer/id #uuid "4fe68738-1add-5c51-b2f9-83c9eca8db5b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{250}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad881ae7-fad5-5cfc-afce-bd920e92937c" :answer/id #uuid "ad881ae7-fad5-5cfc-afce-bd920e92937c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{9x}{125}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7fee2a5e-fafd-50c8-b51c-2713c379fc2f" :answer/id #uuid "7fee2a5e-fafd-50c8-b51c-2713c379fc2f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{9x}{250}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0b428a97-41e2-590a-aace-24167e2b3de2" :answer/id #uuid "0b428a97-41e2-590a-aace-24167e2b3de2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{125}{9{x}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f31e6fee-19f2-52ae-9c5f-033e7ed7a3be" :answer/id #uuid "f31e6fee-19f2-52ae-9c5f-033e7ed7a3be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{125{x}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186111823 :tutorial/content "The **power of quotient rule** states that if we want to raise the quotient $\\dfrac{x}{y}$ to some power $n,$ we must raise the numerator and denominator to the power $n$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{x}{y}\\right)^n=\\dfrac{x^n}{y^n}\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{x}{y}\\right)^4=\\dfrac{x^4}{y^4}.\n$$\n\nTo see why the power of quotient rule is true, let's take the above example and work it out again the long way. We get the same result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{x}{y})}^{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{x}{y}\\cdot \\frac{x}{y}\\cdot \\frac{x}{y}\\cdot \\frac{x}{y} & = \\\\ \\frac{x\\cdot x\\cdot x\\cdot x}{y\\cdot y\\cdot y\\cdot y} & = \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe power of quotient rule is just a shortcut for the process above." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110819 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186197570 :answer-field/choices 17592186197571] [:db/retract 17592186197570 :answer-field/choices 17592186197572] [:db/retract 17592186197570 :answer-field/choices 17592186197573] [:db/retract 17592186197570 :answer-field/choices 17592186197574] [:db/retract 17592186197570 :answer-field/choices 17592186197575] {:db/id 17592186198361 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e9c37ee3-304c-5283-97a5-75391f3d1e94" "question-c5c02db8-6f5e-5ac6-a24e-9e9016b1082e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-e9c37ee3-304c-5283-97a5-75391f3d1e94" :question/id #uuid "e9c37ee3-304c-5283-97a5-75391f3d1e94" :question/math-academy-id "q-82985" :question/problem "Using the change of variables $u=x+3y$ and $v=x+2y,$ evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{x+3y}{x+2y}\\,dx\\,dy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x+3y\\le 2,\\,2\\le x+2y\\le 4\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+3y,\\,v=x+2y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,2\\le v\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x+3y}{x+2y}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\cdot |-1|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{u}{v}\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}u[{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}u[\\operatorname{ln}⁡v]_{2}^{4}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}u(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\\,du \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2){\\int }_{0}^{2}u\\,du \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b260735c-8ce2-5820-82bd-e9db5e7533a8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b260735c-8ce2-5820-82bd-e9db5e7533a8" :answer-field/id #uuid "b260735c-8ce2-5820-82bd-e9db5e7533a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-143bd49e-b2ca-5b56-9eca-6d7751d7c1e3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3b9d745e-3b7a-5566-acd4-df51a55a0434" "answer-143bd49e-b2ca-5b56-9eca-6d7751d7c1e3" "answer-757701d0-e96c-58b7-9b6d-52ab1067ccc8" "answer-37392f66-38d1-5cf1-a83a-b61ffbecdc24" "answer-f8b8b11d-0fcd-5a7a-8d95-67c9db317553"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3b9d745e-3b7a-5566-acd4-df51a55a0434" :answer/id #uuid "3b9d745e-3b7a-5566-acd4-df51a55a0434" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-143bd49e-b2ca-5b56-9eca-6d7751d7c1e3" :answer/id #uuid "143bd49e-b2ca-5b56-9eca-6d7751d7c1e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-757701d0-e96c-58b7-9b6d-52ab1067ccc8" :answer/id #uuid "757701d0-e96c-58b7-9b6d-52ab1067ccc8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-37392f66-38d1-5cf1-a83a-b61ffbecdc24" :answer/id #uuid "37392f66-38d1-5cf1-a83a-b61ffbecdc24" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f8b8b11d-0fcd-5a7a-8d95-67c9db317553" :answer/id #uuid "f8b8b11d-0fcd-5a7a-8d95-67c9db317553" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c5c02db8-6f5e-5ac6-a24e-9e9016b1082e" :question/id #uuid "c5c02db8-6f5e-5ac6-a24e-9e9016b1082e" :question/math-academy-id "q-160899" :question/problem "Using the change of variables $u=\\frac{y}{x}$ and $v=xy,$ evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{e}^{-xy/2}\\,dx\\,dy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{x}{4}\\le y\\le 2x,\\,1\\le xy\\le 4\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{x},\\,v=xy\n$$\n\n \ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ y & \\text{ }\\text{ }x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-\\frac{2y}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{2y}{x})}=-\\frac{1}{2u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{e}^{-xy/2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{-v/2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{-v/2}\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{2u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{1/4}^{2}\\frac{{e}^{-v/2}}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{-v/2}[{\\int }_{1/4}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u]\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{-v/2}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|\\,{|}_{1/4}^{2}]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{-v/2}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{1}{4}]\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{-v/2}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}\\cdot {[-2{e}^{-v/2}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot ({e}^{-2}-{e}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =3({e}^{-1/2}-{e}^{-2})\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-15ea503e-c96f-567a-8f89-cf26cc3e1193"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-15ea503e-c96f-567a-8f89-cf26cc3e1193" :answer-field/id #uuid "15ea503e-c96f-567a-8f89-cf26cc3e1193" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0b6b5fe5-29d1-5793-a274-fa3b5699e913" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e162164d-9035-55fa-9f1c-b47ea0bae241" "answer-0b6b5fe5-29d1-5793-a274-fa3b5699e913" "answer-857734df-e10d-5568-a049-4e87b8b5c0d1" "answer-17dcff5e-6574-5e2e-be4c-22c67c1a31d5" "answer-702b0bc2-ac10-5437-9f18-9fa877532a9e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e162164d-9035-55fa-9f1c-b47ea0bae241" :answer/id #uuid "e162164d-9035-55fa-9f1c-b47ea0bae241" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "({e}^{2}-e)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0b6b5fe5-29d1-5793-a274-fa3b5699e913" :answer/id #uuid "0b6b5fe5-29d1-5793-a274-fa3b5699e913" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3({e}^{-1/2}-{e}^{-2})\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-857734df-e10d-5568-a049-4e87b8b5c0d1" :answer/id #uuid "857734df-e10d-5568-a049-4e87b8b5c0d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8{e}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-17dcff5e-6574-5e2e-be4c-22c67c1a31d5" :answer/id #uuid "17dcff5e-6574-5e2e-be4c-22c67c1a31d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{e}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-702b0bc2-ac10-5437-9f18-9fa877532a9e" :answer/id #uuid "702b0bc2-ac10-5437-9f18-9fa877532a9e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-1}-{e}^{-2}}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186375495 :question/problem "Consider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le y-x\\le 1,\\,0\\le x+y\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables $u=y-x$ and $v=x+y,$ the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}8xy\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u$ and $v$ as" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=y-x,\\,v=x+y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nIn this case, we first need to solve the transformation equations\n\n$$\nu=y-x,\\,v=x+y\n$$\n\n \nfor $x$ and $y$, which gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{v-u}{2},\\,y=\\frac{v+u}{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}8xy\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}8(\\frac{v-u}{2})(\\frac{v+u}{2})|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}2({v}^{2}-{u}^{2})|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}2({v}^{2}-{u}^{2})|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({v}^{2}-{u}^{2})\\,du\\,dv.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198950 :question/problem "Using the change of variables $u=xy$ and $v=y+x,$ evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}|y-x|\\,{e}^{xy}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le xy\\le 4,\\,1\\le y+x\\le 10\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=xy,\\,v=y+x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,1\\le v\\le 10\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}y & x \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =y-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n.\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{y-x}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}|y-x|\\,{e}^{xy}\\,dxdy & ={∬}_{Δ}|y-x|\\,{e}^{u}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|y-x|\\,{e}^{u}\\,\\frac{1}{|y-x|}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}[{\\int }_{1}^{10}{e}^{u}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{u}[{\\int }_{1}^{10}dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{u}[v]_{1}^{10}\\,du \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{u}\\,du \\\\  & =9[{e}^{u}]_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =9({e}^{4}-e).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186197570 :answer-field/correct "answer-04f86c71-5ba2-5262-9bbf-7f7163efa286" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f587b155-dbaf-5156-98a6-ca8c0ee16997" "answer-c84e1d3d-5e71-590c-befa-918bc686cd64" "answer-d9cbdccf-0d8d-5a1e-bca5-5206ced248f1" "answer-3e6403cc-10a5-5873-b7f2-714dc7812c93" "answer-04f86c71-5ba2-5262-9bbf-7f7163efa286"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f587b155-dbaf-5156-98a6-ca8c0ee16997" :answer/id #uuid "f587b155-dbaf-5156-98a6-ca8c0ee16997" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10({e}^{4}-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c84e1d3d-5e71-590c-befa-918bc686cd64" :answer/id #uuid "c84e1d3d-5e71-590c-befa-918bc686cd64" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5(1-{e}^{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d9cbdccf-0d8d-5a1e-bca5-5206ced248f1" :answer/id #uuid "d9cbdccf-0d8d-5a1e-bca5-5206ced248f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5({e}^{4}-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3e6403cc-10a5-5873-b7f2-714dc7812c93" :answer/id #uuid "3e6403cc-10a5-5873-b7f2-714dc7812c93" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9({e}^{3}-e)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-04f86c71-5ba2-5262-9bbf-7f7163efa286" :answer/id #uuid "04f86c71-5ba2-5262-9bbf-7f7163efa286" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9({e}^{4}-e)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119961 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9e1e6dba-6746-5b7e-9ed7-77e1ee7e8c76"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-9e1e6dba-6746-5b7e-9ed7-77e1ee7e8c76" :question/id #uuid "9e1e6dba-6746-5b7e-9ed7-77e1ee7e8c76" :question/math-academy-id "q-71494" :question/problem "$(\\sqrt[3]{1,000})^{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "The cube and the cube root cancel each other out. So, we have\n\n$$\n(\\sqrt[3]{1,000})^{3}=1,000.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7a4987cd-b000-5d3d-b94d-9c8d5cd48460"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7a4987cd-b000-5d3d-b94d-9c8d5cd48460" :answer-field/id #uuid "7a4987cd-b000-5d3d-b94d-9c8d5cd48460" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8b0b316d-8f3a-5a45-a97f-136faefce7bc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5c730b9a-1755-532b-b8d1-24d3aece98b2" "answer-f015c16e-7bc0-5eb8-9649-05d8fe751886" "answer-8b0b316d-8f3a-5a45-a97f-136faefce7bc" "answer-11db075b-d65e-51e5-8dca-f8e9ab1adb79" "answer-671ff086-c9db-58f6-af7a-4edb329a1ac6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5c730b9a-1755-532b-b8d1-24d3aece98b2" :answer/id #uuid "5c730b9a-1755-532b-b8d1-24d3aece98b2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f015c16e-7bc0-5eb8-9649-05d8fe751886" :answer/id #uuid "f015c16e-7bc0-5eb8-9649-05d8fe751886" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b0b316d-8f3a-5a45-a97f-136faefce7bc" :answer/id #uuid "8b0b316d-8f3a-5a45-a97f-136faefce7bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-11db075b-d65e-51e5-8dca-f8e9ab1adb79" :answer/id #uuid "11db075b-d65e-51e5-8dca-f8e9ab1adb79" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-671ff086-c9db-58f6-af7a-4edb329a1ac6" :answer/id #uuid "671ff086-c9db-58f6-af7a-4edb329a1ac6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "100" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119959 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-aafcb24f-fde2-52b2-b44a-c7d7fddbc683" "question-03b02208-a47d-5484-9a1d-0e9d1a5e06da"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-aafcb24f-fde2-52b2-b44a-c7d7fddbc683" :question/id #uuid "aafcb24f-fde2-52b2-b44a-c7d7fddbc683" :question/math-academy-id "q-186155" :question/problem "Evaluate the following:\n\n$\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{27}{8}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the numbers $27$ and $8$ are both perfect cubes. \n\nSo, to determine the value of $\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{27}{8}},$ we express $\\frac{27}{8}$ as a perfect cube:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{27}{8}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{3}^{3}}{{2}^{3}}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{3\\cdot 3\\cdot 3}{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f90a68c7-dc57-5362-b958-0dc11f3f0c40"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f90a68c7-dc57-5362-b958-0dc11f3f0c40" :answer-field/id #uuid "f90a68c7-dc57-5362-b958-0dc11f3f0c40" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-aa5916f5-b95b-5530-9d9f-19d0168e2209" :answer-field/choices ["answer-aa5916f5-b95b-5530-9d9f-19d0168e2209"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aa5916f5-b95b-5530-9d9f-19d0168e2209" :answer/id #uuid "aa5916f5-b95b-5530-9d9f-19d0168e2209" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-03b02208-a47d-5484-9a1d-0e9d1a5e06da" :question/id #uuid "03b02208-a47d-5484-9a1d-0e9d1a5e06da" :question/math-academy-id "q-186156" :question/problem "Evaluate the following:\n\n$\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{125}{8}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the numbers $125$ and $8$ are both perfect cubes. \n\nSo, to determine the value of $\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{125}{8}},$ we express $\\frac{125}{8}$ as a perfect cube:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{125}{8}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{5}^{3}}{{2}^{3}}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5}{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-77156935-a293-5f77-b17e-01ad81fabeda"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-77156935-a293-5f77-b17e-01ad81fabeda" :answer-field/id #uuid "77156935-a293-5f77-b17e-01ad81fabeda" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-2cbd15cf-50e0-5f6c-a023-66b7777a5ea1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2cbd15cf-50e0-5f6c-a023-66b7777a5ea1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2cbd15cf-50e0-5f6c-a023-66b7777a5ea1" :answer/id #uuid "2cbd15cf-50e0-5f6c-a023-66b7777a5ea1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-9b8dddd1-da8a-515b-b440-7da08015b94d" :activity/id #uuid "9b8dddd1-da8a-515b-b440-7da08015b94d" :activity/title "The Cube Root of a Perfect Cube" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048770 :activity/course 17592186046648 :activity/steps ["step-3b1fdc86-b563-5301-a67d-cba49bff52be" "step-d4a52c70-3918-5a00-b200-12e071807393" "step-734e7353-a71f-5a59-9552-8bb9d82e349e"] :activity/first-step "step-3b1fdc86-b563-5301-a67d-cba49bff52be" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-3b1fdc86-b563-5301-a67d-cba49bff52be" :step/id #uuid "3b1fdc86-b563-5301-a67d-cba49bff52be" :step/content "question-9e1e6dba-6746-5b7e-9ed7-77e1ee7e8c76" :step/next "step-d4a52c70-3918-5a00-b200-12e071807393" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d4a52c70-3918-5a00-b200-12e071807393" :step/id #uuid "d4a52c70-3918-5a00-b200-12e071807393" :step/content "question-aafcb24f-fde2-52b2-b44a-c7d7fddbc683" :step/next "step-734e7353-a71f-5a59-9552-8bb9d82e349e" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-734e7353-a71f-5a59-9552-8bb9d82e349e" :step/id #uuid "734e7353-a71f-5a59-9552-8bb9d82e349e" :step/content "question-03b02208-a47d-5484-9a1d-0e9d1a5e06da" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064736 :question/problem "What is the coefficient of ${x}^{2}y$ in the product $(x-y)({x}^{2}+6xy-9{y}^{2})?$" :question/worked-solution "We distribute all three terms of the binomial in the first set of parentheses to the trinomial in the second set of parentheses. Then, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-y)({x}^{2}+6xy-9{y}^{2}) & = \\\\ x({x}^{2}+6xy-9{y}^{2})+(-y)({x}^{2}+6xy-9{y}^{2}) & = \\\\ {x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}y-9x{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}y-6x{y}^{2}+9{y}^{3} & = \\\\ {x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}y-15x{y}^{2}+9{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of ${x}^{2}y$ is $5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186062473 :answer-field/correct 17592186062477 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064277 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d9aae8a4-653e-5752-9c3b-a882c899846f" "question-222f2e72-f895-5b79-8f06-000077812dd5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-d9aae8a4-653e-5752-9c3b-a882c899846f" :question/id #uuid "d9aae8a4-653e-5752-9c3b-a882c899846f" :question/math-academy-id "q-78170" :question/problem "Given that  $({p}^{2}+p+1)({p}^{2}+3p+1)={p}^{4}+a{p}^{3}+b{p}^{2}+cp+1,$ calculate $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "We distribute all three terms of the trinomial in the first set of parentheses to the trinomial in the second set of parentheses. Then, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({p}^{2}+p+1)({p}^{2}+3p+1) & = \\\\ {p}^{2}({p}^{2}+3p+1)+p({p}^{2}+3p+1)+1({p}^{2}+3p+1) & = \\\\ {p}^{4}+3{p}^{3}+{p}^{2}+{p}^{3}+3{p}^{2}+p+{p}^{2}+3p+1 & = \\\\ {p}^{4}+4{p}^{3}+5{p}^{2}+4p+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=4,$ $b=5,$ and $c=4.$ So, \n\n$$\na+b+c=4+5+4=13.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d4c94381-cca8-57b8-bdf5-3cd85a1f90d0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d4c94381-cca8-57b8-bdf5-3cd85a1f90d0" :answer-field/id #uuid "d4c94381-cca8-57b8-bdf5-3cd85a1f90d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e27c97bd-9bdc-5a82-9295-1903f7e28d23" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e8b629bc-bc84-51ab-bcdf-5cf5e9435232" "answer-e27c97bd-9bdc-5a82-9295-1903f7e28d23" "answer-1509f49e-da21-5888-a3e2-156f6abd8874" "answer-45f89047-5bab-5a07-8abc-c0436c6ee8a9" "answer-39d6163e-8a28-5dea-acdf-43f43b916822"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e8b629bc-bc84-51ab-bcdf-5cf5e9435232" :answer/id #uuid "e8b629bc-bc84-51ab-bcdf-5cf5e9435232" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e27c97bd-9bdc-5a82-9295-1903f7e28d23" :answer/id #uuid "e27c97bd-9bdc-5a82-9295-1903f7e28d23" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "13" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1509f49e-da21-5888-a3e2-156f6abd8874" :answer/id #uuid "1509f49e-da21-5888-a3e2-156f6abd8874" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45f89047-5bab-5a07-8abc-c0436c6ee8a9" :answer/id #uuid "45f89047-5bab-5a07-8abc-c0436c6ee8a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-39d6163e-8a28-5dea-acdf-43f43b916822" :answer/id #uuid "39d6163e-8a28-5dea-acdf-43f43b916822" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-222f2e72-f895-5b79-8f06-000077812dd5" :question/id #uuid "222f2e72-f895-5b79-8f06-000077812dd5" :question/math-academy-id "q-78171" :question/problem "Given that  $({t}^{2}+t+2)({t}^{2}+2t+1)={t}^{4}+a{t}^{3}+b{t}^{2}+ct+2,$ calculate $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "We distribute all three terms of the trinomial in the first set of parentheses to the trinomial in the second set of parentheses. Then, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({t}^{2}+t+2)({t}^{2}+2t+1) & = \\\\ {t}^{2}({t}^{2}+2t+1)+t({t}^{2}+2t+1)+2({t}^{2}+2t+1) & = \\\\ {t}^{4}+2{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}+{t}^{3}+2{t}^{2}+t+2{t}^{2}+4t+2 & = \\\\ {t}^{4}+3{t}^{3}+5{t}^{2}+5t+2 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=3,$ $b=5,$ and $c=5.$ So, \n\n$$\na+b+c=3+5+5=13.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fb22af84-6e18-5551-a3f1-bbcb3eb8cfb6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fb22af84-6e18-5551-a3f1-bbcb3eb8cfb6" :answer-field/id #uuid "fb22af84-6e18-5551-a3f1-bbcb3eb8cfb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6186a0e8-15ad-5f04-9514-03a897bd46d1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-79daec01-1bd7-52c2-9729-762b7fa0a1f5" "answer-89b4a778-8744-54f1-9b64-0291ebefd5aa" "answer-6186a0e8-15ad-5f04-9514-03a897bd46d1" "answer-db0402ea-ca02-5133-8b2b-0f9106ee55f8" "answer-7bcf2e2e-64a8-5b8a-80c5-fd2e2740d5d8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-79daec01-1bd7-52c2-9729-762b7fa0a1f5" :answer/id #uuid "79daec01-1bd7-52c2-9729-762b7fa0a1f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-89b4a778-8744-54f1-9b64-0291ebefd5aa" :answer/id #uuid "89b4a778-8744-54f1-9b64-0291ebefd5aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6186a0e8-15ad-5f04-9514-03a897bd46d1" :answer/id #uuid "6186a0e8-15ad-5f04-9514-03a897bd46d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "13" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-db0402ea-ca02-5133-8b2b-0f9106ee55f8" :answer/id #uuid "db0402ea-ca02-5133-8b2b-0f9106ee55f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7bcf2e2e-64a8-5b8a-80c5-fd2e2740d5d8" :answer/id #uuid "7bcf2e2e-64a8-5b8a-80c5-fd2e2740d5d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-7d34f865-13dc-53b8-9562-0158bac35795" :activity/id #uuid "7d34f865-13dc-53b8-9562-0158bac35795" :activity/title "Multiplying Polynomials" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049500 :activity/course 17592186046648 :activity/steps ["step-01cd63b9-1bc2-5c63-877d-2c84f5a29f2f" "step-9f30599b-eb03-56eb-8b41-df7c06969977" "step-97cc7e1e-8c02-52bb-8813-52ea962add8a"] :activity/first-step "step-01cd63b9-1bc2-5c63-877d-2c84f5a29f2f" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-01cd63b9-1bc2-5c63-877d-2c84f5a29f2f" :step/id #uuid "01cd63b9-1bc2-5c63-877d-2c84f5a29f2f" :step/content 17592186064736 :step/next "step-9f30599b-eb03-56eb-8b41-df7c06969977" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-9f30599b-eb03-56eb-8b41-df7c06969977" :step/id #uuid "9f30599b-eb03-56eb-8b41-df7c06969977" :step/content "question-d9aae8a4-653e-5752-9c3b-a882c899846f" :step/next "step-97cc7e1e-8c02-52bb-8813-52ea962add8a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-97cc7e1e-8c02-52bb-8813-52ea962add8a" :step/id #uuid "97cc7e1e-8c02-52bb-8813-52ea962add8a" :step/content "question-222f2e72-f895-5b79-8f06-000077812dd5" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186169161 :answer-field/choices 17592186169162] [:db/retract 17592186169161 :answer-field/choices 17592186169163] [:db/retract 17592186169161 :answer-field/choices 17592186169164] [:db/retract 17592186169161 :answer-field/choices 17592186169165] [:db/retract 17592186169161 :answer-field/choices 17592186169166] {:db/id 17592186172135 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9d5803ec-f126-5445-94a3-4045259deba2" "question-3e1bedf1-a88d-51f4-8239-18dd7d6b26f1" "question-9aa1e55d-7de9-53b4-a8d5-5393a30ab5e5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171717 :question/problem "Suppose we want to find the minimum of the function $f(x,y,z)=2xz+y^2$ on the intersection of the surfaces $x-z=2$ and $x^2+y^2+2z^2=3$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$ satisfy the following system: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨2z,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,2x⟩=\\lambda ⟨1,\\,0,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,\\,2y,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩ \\\\ x-z=2 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the expressions?" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = 2xz+y^2\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x-z=2, \\qquad h(x,y,z)=x^2+y^2+2z^2=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=2 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now expand the first equation of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2z,\\,2y,\\,2x⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,\\,0,\\,-1⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,\\,2y,\\,4z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing parts are $2y,\\:$ $-1,\\:$ and $4z.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172134 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4d8f93db-ca73-5128-94a3-1e0c56cdcf95" "question-90c2cf43-9c0a-5736-b63a-0ceceb8b2660" "question-daa44e18-10db-5d53-b4fb-045b6b617679" "question-e3b568f7-cd27-5a4c-97e4-f5b7033e39b7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171716 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) =   x-y-z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x+y+z=2, \\qquad h(x,y,z)=x^2 + z^2 = 4.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=2 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,1,1⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,0,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}1=\\lambda +2\\mu x \\\\ -1=\\lambda  \\\\ -1=\\lambda +2\\mu z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{\\mu } \\\\ \\lambda =-1 \\\\ \\mu z=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu$ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction. So, from the third equation above, we must have $z=0.$\n\nNext, we substitute $z=0$  into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we substitute $x$ and $z$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $x=2$ and $z= 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =2 \\\\ 2+y+0 & =2 \\\\ 2+y & =2 \\\\ y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=-2$ and $z=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =2 \\\\ -2+y+0 & =2 \\\\ -2+y & =2 \\\\ y & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(2, 0, 0), \\qquad (-2, 4, 0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(2, 0, 0 )$ | $(-2, 4, 0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $2$ | $-6$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-2, 4, 0),$ and its minimum value is $-6.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172383 :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find the maximum of the function $f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}z$ on the intersection of the surfaces ${x}^{2}-2{z}^{2}=1$ and ${y}^{2}+3{z}^{3}=5$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\\lambda $ and $\\mu $ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨{y}^{2}z,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,x{y}^{2}⟩=\\lambda ⟨2x,\\,0,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩+\\mu ⟨0,\\,2y,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩ \\\\ {x}^{2}-2{z}^{2}=1 \\\\ {y}^{2}+3{z}^{3}=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the expressions?" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the maximum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}z\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)={x}^{2}-2{z}^{2}=1,\\,h(x,y,z)={y}^{2}+3{z}^{3}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=1 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now expand the first equation of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨{y}^{2}z,\\,2xyz,\\,x{y}^{2}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,\\,0,\\,-4z⟩+\\mu ⟨0,\\,2y,\\,9{z}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186169161 :answer-field/correct "answer-13bd14f4-f8f2-5425-a0c0-c742d2dc526f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-70c1f0ea-b5d8-5438-ae74-3be3c1d22bc9" "answer-3871d77e-1936-5111-acda-0de64b6b51e6" "answer-24a1696a-668d-5ef0-ba9e-c76867e73917" "answer-13bd14f4-f8f2-5425-a0c0-c742d2dc526f" "answer-6aa42d21-1c63-53d2-bde4-63b3f0f352e5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-70c1f0ea-b5d8-5438-ae74-3be3c1d22bc9" :answer/id #uuid "70c1f0ea-b5d8-5438-ae74-3be3c1d22bc9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$-2y,\\,$ $-4z,\\,$ and $3{z}^{2}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3871d77e-1936-5111-acda-0de64b6b51e6" :answer/id #uuid "3871d77e-1936-5111-acda-0de64b6b51e6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$-2yz,\\,$ $2x,\\,$ and $3{z}^{3}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-24a1696a-668d-5ef0-ba9e-c76867e73917" :answer/id #uuid "24a1696a-668d-5ef0-ba9e-c76867e73917" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$2xyz,\\,$ $-2{z}^{2},\\,$ and $3{z}^{2}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-13bd14f4-f8f2-5425-a0c0-c742d2dc526f" :answer/id #uuid "13bd14f4-f8f2-5425-a0c0-c742d2dc526f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$2xyz,\\,$ $-4z,\\,$ and $9{z}^{2}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6aa42d21-1c63-53d2-bde4-63b3f0f352e5" :answer/id #uuid "6aa42d21-1c63-53d2-bde4-63b3f0f352e5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${y}^{2}z,\\,$ $-{z}^{2},\\,$ and $3{z}^{2}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9d5803ec-f126-5445-94a3-4045259deba2" :question/id #uuid "9d5803ec-f126-5445-94a3-4045259deba2" :question/math-academy-id "q-162814" :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find the minimum of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}$ on the intersection of the surfaces ${y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4$ and $2x-y+{z}^{2}=4$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\\lambda $ and $\\mu $ satisfy the following system: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨2x,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,-2z⟩=\\lambda ⟨0,\\,2y,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩+\\mu ⟨2,\\,-1,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩ \\\\ {y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4 \\\\ 2x-y+{z}^{2}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the expressions?" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)={y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4,\\,h(x,y,z)=2x-y+{z}^{2}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=4 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now expand the first equation of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,\\,4y,\\,-2z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨0,\\,2y,\\,2z⟩+\\mu ⟨2,\\,-1,\\,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ab363583-7f08-5d8c-81f8-2a64b51d7010"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ab363583-7f08-5d8c-81f8-2a64b51d7010" :answer-field/id #uuid "ab363583-7f08-5d8c-81f8-2a64b51d7010" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-637782f8-5060-58aa-b1ac-cd0c5892b07e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ee237b50-6e80-527a-87ec-af3f5a3a9282" "answer-5850d96f-cd3a-52af-b926-0eb94f43b034" "answer-9765660b-c840-5458-b02f-de5fbb17e64c" "answer-637782f8-5060-58aa-b1ac-cd0c5892b07e" "answer-48267671-3c22-599a-906d-15947ab84541"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ee237b50-6e80-527a-87ec-af3f5a3a9282" :answer/id #uuid "ee237b50-6e80-527a-87ec-af3f5a3a9282" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$-2z,\\,$ $x,\\,$ and $2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5850d96f-cd3a-52af-b926-0eb94f43b034" :answer/id #uuid "5850d96f-cd3a-52af-b926-0eb94f43b034" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$2x,\\,$ $-1,\\,$ and $x$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9765660b-c840-5458-b02f-de5fbb17e64c" :answer/id #uuid "9765660b-c840-5458-b02f-de5fbb17e64c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$4y,\\,$ $1,\\,$ and $-2z$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-637782f8-5060-58aa-b1ac-cd0c5892b07e" :answer/id #uuid "637782f8-5060-58aa-b1ac-cd0c5892b07e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$4y,\\,$ $2z,\\,$ and $2z$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-48267671-3c22-599a-906d-15947ab84541" :answer/id #uuid "48267671-3c22-599a-906d-15947ab84541" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y,\\,$ $2,\\,$ and $1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3e1bedf1-a88d-51f4-8239-18dd7d6b26f1" :question/id #uuid "3e1bedf1-a88d-51f4-8239-18dd7d6b26f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-162809" :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=xy+{z}^{2}$ on the intersection of the surfaces ${z}^{2}+{x}^{2}=4$ and $x+2y=-2$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\\lambda $ and $\\mu $ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨y,\\,x,\\,2z⟩=\\lambda ⟨\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,0,\\,2z⟩+\\mu ⟨1,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,0⟩ \\\\ {z}^{2}+{x}^{2}=4 \\\\ x+2y=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing expressions?" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy+{z}^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)={z}^{2}+{x}^{2}=4,\\,h(x,y,z)=x+2y=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=4 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now expand the first equation of the system: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨y,\\,x,\\,2z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,\\,0,\\,2z⟩+\\mu ⟨1,\\,2,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a852ffd1-8b11-5f79-833e-b65af8fa8346"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a852ffd1-8b11-5f79-833e-b65af8fa8346" :answer-field/id #uuid "a852ffd1-8b11-5f79-833e-b65af8fa8346" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-80a1b39c-24bc-5f2c-84e5-1b4dc7369d3c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9013aefb-f1d5-51f5-b261-83ccbece62c8" "answer-062a0d8b-1164-510a-b878-42ee11980575" "answer-80a1b39c-24bc-5f2c-84e5-1b4dc7369d3c" "answer-b03b5d8d-43ac-5b85-807e-fd77d3684d6a" "answer-c35373e7-274c-52a1-8823-254d1f74b230"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9013aefb-f1d5-51f5-b261-83ccbece62c8" :answer/id #uuid "9013aefb-f1d5-51f5-b261-83ccbece62c8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$-1\\,$ and $x$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-062a0d8b-1164-510a-b878-42ee11980575" :answer/id #uuid "062a0d8b-1164-510a-b878-42ee11980575" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$-2\\,$ and $y$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-80a1b39c-24bc-5f2c-84e5-1b4dc7369d3c" :answer/id #uuid "80a1b39c-24bc-5f2c-84e5-1b4dc7369d3c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$2x\\,$ and $2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b03b5d8d-43ac-5b85-807e-fd77d3684d6a" :answer/id #uuid "b03b5d8d-43ac-5b85-807e-fd77d3684d6a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$2z\\,$ and $-1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c35373e7-274c-52a1-8823-254d1f74b230" :answer/id #uuid "c35373e7-274c-52a1-8823-254d1f74b230" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y\\,$ and $-2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9aa1e55d-7de9-53b4-a8d5-5393a30ab5e5" :question/id #uuid "9aa1e55d-7de9-53b4-a8d5-5393a30ab5e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-162816" :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find the maximum of the function $f(x,y,z)=3x+2z+{y}^{2}$ on the intersection of the surfaces ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}=1$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\\lambda $ and $\\mu $ satisfy the following system: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨3,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,2⟩=\\lambda ⟨2x,\\,2y,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩+\\mu ⟨4x,\\,2y,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩ \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the expressions?" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the maximum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=3x+2z+{y}^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,\\,h(x,y,z)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=1 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now expand the first equation of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨3,\\,2y,\\,2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,\\,2y,\\,0⟩+\\mu ⟨4x,\\,2y,\\,8z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6f22b591-30cd-52f7-a24e-bb816c9f1346"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6f22b591-30cd-52f7-a24e-bb816c9f1346" :answer-field/id #uuid "6f22b591-30cd-52f7-a24e-bb816c9f1346" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7d084b5c-a828-5df4-9946-4103e1f34b0a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-95cf3ed2-0d12-5a39-bf5c-97ada95a469b" "answer-16c8a2b1-da85-5994-b78c-c9e95612a9a2" "answer-df044209-61e8-50cd-9db7-ccaaa780982c" "answer-7d084b5c-a828-5df4-9946-4103e1f34b0a" "answer-e107cf08-1118-512e-98ba-d7f6aff904ec"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-95cf3ed2-0d12-5a39-bf5c-97ada95a469b" :answer/id #uuid "95cf3ed2-0d12-5a39-bf5c-97ada95a469b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$-2y,\\,$ $0,\\,$ and ${z}^{2}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-16c8a2b1-da85-5994-b78c-c9e95612a9a2" :answer/id #uuid "16c8a2b1-da85-5994-b78c-c9e95612a9a2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$2,\\,$ $0,\\,$ and $2z$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df044209-61e8-50cd-9db7-ccaaa780982c" :answer/id #uuid "df044209-61e8-50cd-9db7-ccaaa780982c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$2,\\,$ $1,\\,$ and $4z$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d084b5c-a828-5df4-9946-4103e1f34b0a" :answer/id #uuid "7d084b5c-a828-5df4-9946-4103e1f34b0a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$2y,\\,$ $0,\\,$ and $8z$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e107cf08-1118-512e-98ba-d7f6aff904ec" :answer/id #uuid "e107cf08-1118-512e-98ba-d7f6aff904ec" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${y}^{2},\\,$ $0,\\,$ and $4{z}^{2}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4d8f93db-ca73-5128-94a3-1e0c56cdcf95" :question/id #uuid "4d8f93db-ca73-5128-94a3-1e0c56cdcf95" :question/math-academy-id "q-162894" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=x+2y+3z$ on the intersection of the plane $x-y-z=0$ and the cylinder $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the maximum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x+2y+3z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x-y-z=0,\\,h(x,y,z)=4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=0 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,2,3⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,-1,-1⟩+\\mu ⟨8x,2y,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}1=\\lambda +8\\mu x \\\\ 2=-\\lambda +2\\mu y \\\\ 3=-\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{2\\mu } \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{2\\mu } \\\\ \\lambda =-3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu $ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction.\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{\\mu }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\mu }^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4{\\mu }^{2}} & =1 \\\\ {\\mu }^{2} & =\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ \\mu  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},\\,y=∓\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x$ and $y$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}$ and $y=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y-z & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}-(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}})-z & =0 \\\\ z & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}$ and $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y-z & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}-(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}})-z & =0 \\\\ z & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}),\\,(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}})\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}})$ | $(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $\\sqrt{5}$ | $-\\sqrt{5}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}),$ and its   maximum value is $\\sqrt{5}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c5176422-5204-53ed-afa1-9ce312f9aff4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c5176422-5204-53ed-afa1-9ce312f9aff4" :answer-field/id #uuid "c5176422-5204-53ed-afa1-9ce312f9aff4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4ef2a3c3-778f-5c55-8969-0e9611df5e89" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e985e777-05bb-502e-8fdf-908a7f9d009f" "answer-a9f98c30-eef0-520b-8077-2c5bc53b8e99" "answer-126577ac-3152-598b-9fe4-040ffdbae6e8" "answer-4ef2a3c3-778f-5c55-8969-0e9611df5e89" "answer-79e1cfa8-b203-5b99-beca-b7ab609085ed"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e985e777-05bb-502e-8fdf-908a7f9d009f" :answer/id #uuid "e985e777-05bb-502e-8fdf-908a7f9d009f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a9f98c30-eef0-520b-8077-2c5bc53b8e99" :answer/id #uuid "a9f98c30-eef0-520b-8077-2c5bc53b8e99" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-126577ac-3152-598b-9fe4-040ffdbae6e8" :answer/id #uuid "126577ac-3152-598b-9fe4-040ffdbae6e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4ef2a3c3-778f-5c55-8969-0e9611df5e89" :answer/id #uuid "4ef2a3c3-778f-5c55-8969-0e9611df5e89" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-79e1cfa8-b203-5b99-beca-b7ab609085ed" :answer/id #uuid "79e1cfa8-b203-5b99-beca-b7ab609085ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172382 :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=2x-2y+z$ on the intersection of the plane $x-y+z=1$ and the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=2x-2y+z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x-y+z=1,\\,h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=1 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,-2,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,-1,1⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,2y,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2=\\lambda +2\\mu x \\\\ -2=-\\lambda +2\\mu y \\\\ 1=\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{2\\mu } \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{2\\mu } \\\\ \\lambda =1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu $ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction.\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4{\\mu }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\mu }^{2}} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2{\\mu }^{2}} & =2 \\\\ {\\mu }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\mu  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=\\pm 1,\\,y=∓1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x$ and $y$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $x=1$ and $y=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y+z & =1 \\\\ 1-(-1)+z & =1 \\\\ 2+z & =1 \\\\ z & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=-1$ and $y=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y+z & =1 \\\\ -1-1+z & =1 \\\\ -2+z & =1 \\\\ z & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(1,-1,-1),\\,(-1,1,3).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(1,-1,-1)$ | $(-1,1,3)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $3$ | $-1$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-1,1,3),$ and its minimum value is $-1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186169155 :answer-field/correct 17592186169158 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-90c2cf43-9c0a-5736-b63a-0ceceb8b2660" :question/id #uuid "90c2cf43-9c0a-5736-b63a-0ceceb8b2660" :question/math-academy-id "q-162893" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=4x+y+2z$ on the intersection of the plane $x+y+z=0$ and the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=4x+y+2z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x+y+z=0,\\,h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=0 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨4,1,2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,1,1⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,2y,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}4=\\lambda +2\\mu x \\\\ 1=\\lambda +2\\mu y \\\\ 2=\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{\\mu } \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{2\\mu } \\\\ \\lambda =2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu $ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction.\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{\\mu }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\mu }^{2}} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4{\\mu }^{2}} & =5 \\\\ {\\mu }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\mu  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=\\pm 2,\\,y=∓1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x$ and $y$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $x=2$ and $y=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =0 \\\\ 2+(-1)+z & =0 \\\\ 1+z & =0 \\\\ z & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=-2$ and $y=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =0 \\\\ -2+1+z & =0 \\\\ -1+z & =0 \\\\ z & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(2,-1,-1),\\,(-2,1,1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(2,-1,-1)$ | $(-2,1,1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $5$ | $-5$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-2,1,1),$ and its minimum value is $-5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-38401504-2977-5fc3-a7df-aed6a5b7e6b4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-38401504-2977-5fc3-a7df-aed6a5b7e6b4" :answer-field/id #uuid "38401504-2977-5fc3-a7df-aed6a5b7e6b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b4e91317-8af1-5dfd-a4d3-8c380cd7d717" :answer-field/choices ["answer-18c4cb35-744c-5bb2-be59-05606fbe5ebd" "answer-b4e91317-8af1-5dfd-a4d3-8c380cd7d717" "answer-0005042c-e337-5a0c-a1db-b063bfe254a4" "answer-b3adee24-018e-57fe-bcb0-1019592eb22b" "answer-9a8fefed-a005-5293-b27d-4d1ec085f9f1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-18c4cb35-744c-5bb2-be59-05606fbe5ebd" :answer/id #uuid "18c4cb35-744c-5bb2-be59-05606fbe5ebd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b4e91317-8af1-5dfd-a4d3-8c380cd7d717" :answer/id #uuid "b4e91317-8af1-5dfd-a4d3-8c380cd7d717" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0005042c-e337-5a0c-a1db-b063bfe254a4" :answer/id #uuid "0005042c-e337-5a0c-a1db-b063bfe254a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b3adee24-018e-57fe-bcb0-1019592eb22b" :answer/id #uuid "b3adee24-018e-57fe-bcb0-1019592eb22b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9a8fefed-a005-5293-b27d-4d1ec085f9f1" :answer/id #uuid "9a8fefed-a005-5293-b27d-4d1ec085f9f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-daa44e18-10db-5d53-b4fb-045b6b617679" :question/id #uuid "daa44e18-10db-5d53-b4fb-045b6b617679" :question/math-academy-id "q-162897" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=2x+4y-2z$ on the intersection of the plane $2x-4y-2z=1$ and the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=2x+4y-2z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=2x-4y-2z=1,\\,h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=1 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,4,-2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2,-4,-2⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,0,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2=2\\lambda +2\\mu x \\\\ 4=-4\\lambda  \\\\ -2=-2\\lambda +2\\mu z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{2}{\\mu } \\\\ \\lambda =-1 \\\\ z=-\\frac{2}{\\mu }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu $ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction.\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $z$ into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{4}{{\\mu }^{2}}+\\frac{4}{{\\mu }^{2}} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{8}{{\\mu }^{2}} & =2 \\\\ {\\mu }^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=\\pm 1,\\,z=∓1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x$ and $z$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $x=1$ and $z=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-4y-2z & =1 \\\\ 2(1)-4y-2(-1) & =1 \\\\ -4y & =-3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=-1$ and $z=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-4y-2z & =1 \\\\ 2(-1)-4y-2(1) & =1 \\\\ -4y & =5 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(1,\\frac{3}{4},-1),\\,(-1,-\\frac{5}{4},1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(1,\\frac{3}{4},-1)$ | $(-1,-\\frac{5}{4},1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $7$ | $-9$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-1,-\\frac{5}{4},1),$ and its minimum value is $-9.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-55f0be58-6264-52fc-81bc-9aa7d91e0659"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-55f0be58-6264-52fc-81bc-9aa7d91e0659" :answer-field/id #uuid "55f0be58-6264-52fc-81bc-9aa7d91e0659" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6cc52ef3-233d-50cf-981c-9d55c945e79b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3b33f311-775b-531d-a7c1-211917078fee" "answer-6cc52ef3-233d-50cf-981c-9d55c945e79b" "answer-d635b4ea-d9c6-5d7b-b741-bd02b50aa24f" "answer-fa2baa31-cb4e-5fc8-881b-3a0207f4db3c" "answer-ffea41a4-e1d5-5dcb-a38a-77b3723e6c19"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3b33f311-775b-531d-a7c1-211917078fee" :answer/id #uuid "3b33f311-775b-531d-a7c1-211917078fee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6cc52ef3-233d-50cf-981c-9d55c945e79b" :answer/id #uuid "6cc52ef3-233d-50cf-981c-9d55c945e79b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d635b4ea-d9c6-5d7b-b741-bd02b50aa24f" :answer/id #uuid "d635b4ea-d9c6-5d7b-b741-bd02b50aa24f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa2baa31-cb4e-5fc8-881b-3a0207f4db3c" :answer/id #uuid "fa2baa31-cb4e-5fc8-881b-3a0207f4db3c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ffea41a4-e1d5-5dcb-a38a-77b3723e6c19" :answer/id #uuid "ffea41a4-e1d5-5dcb-a38a-77b3723e6c19" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e3b568f7-cd27-5a4c-97e4-f5b7033e39b7" :question/id #uuid "e3b568f7-cd27-5a4c-97e4-f5b7033e39b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-162830" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=4x+2y-z$ on the intersection of the plane $x-y-z=2$ and the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the maximum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=4x+2y-z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x-y-z=2,\\,h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=2 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨4,2,-1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,-1,-1⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,2y,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}4=\\lambda +2\\mu x \\\\ 2=-\\lambda +2\\mu y \\\\ -1=-\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{3}{2\\mu } \\\\ y=\\frac{3}{2\\mu } \\\\ \\lambda =1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu $ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction.\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{9}{4{\\mu }^{2}}+\\frac{9}{4{\\mu }^{2}} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2{\\mu }^{2}} & =2 \\\\ {\\mu }^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ \\mu  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, since $x=y,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)=(1,1)\\,\\text{or}\\,(x,y)=(-1,-1).\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x$ and $y$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $x=1$ and $y=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y-z & =2 \\\\ 1-1-z & =2 \\\\ z & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=-1$ and $y=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y-z & =2 \\\\ -1-(-1)-z & =2 \\\\ -1+1-z & =2 \\\\ z & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(1,1,-2),\\,(-1,-1,-2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(1,1,-2)$ | $(-1,-1,-2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $8$ | $-4$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(1,1,-2),$ and its maximum value is $8.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-eeb06b96-cbcf-5cb8-b34d-617abe9e3c88"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-eeb06b96-cbcf-5cb8-b34d-617abe9e3c88" :answer-field/id #uuid "eeb06b96-cbcf-5cb8-b34d-617abe9e3c88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e65a77b0-99d4-5d1a-949f-8c30e9525e96" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1a800fbf-d667-517e-b0be-8ddfb0053d6c" "answer-ae0848f9-3de1-5239-ac6f-93ab9d8c261d" "answer-ab8b12f1-7c38-5f23-a15e-1ff64cbf0b80" "answer-7125e289-4617-5f04-9b31-2d782200a923" "answer-e65a77b0-99d4-5d1a-949f-8c30e9525e96"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1a800fbf-d667-517e-b0be-8ddfb0053d6c" :answer/id #uuid "1a800fbf-d667-517e-b0be-8ddfb0053d6c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae0848f9-3de1-5239-ac6f-93ab9d8c261d" :answer/id #uuid "ae0848f9-3de1-5239-ac6f-93ab9d8c261d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ab8b12f1-7c38-5f23-a15e-1ff64cbf0b80" :answer/id #uuid "ab8b12f1-7c38-5f23-a15e-1ff64cbf0b80" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7125e289-4617-5f04-9b31-2d782200a923" :answer/id #uuid "7125e289-4617-5f04-9b31-2d782200a923" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e65a77b0-99d4-5d1a-949f-8c30e9525e96" :answer/id #uuid "e65a77b0-99d4-5d1a-949f-8c30e9525e96" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186173258 :tutorial/content "We can find global extrema of multivariable functions subject to multiple constraints using the method of Lagrange multipliers. \n\nFor example, suppose that we want to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = 4xy+z^2\n$$\n\nsubject to the following *two* constraints: \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x^2+y^2=1, \\qquad h(x,y,z)=2x^2 + 4y^2 - z = 0\n$$\n\nGeometrically, this means that we want to minimize $f(x,y,z)$ over the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=1$ and the elliptic paraboloid $2x^2 + 4y^2 - z = 0,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/0b/0b16f73e034724a9f38d91a606f627db85299507552c45192e91c39bc0c6725a.png)\n\nThe method of Lagrange multipliers states that the extrema of the function $f(x,y,z)$ subject to constraints $g(x,y,z)=a$ and $h(x,y,z)=b$ can be found by solving the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=a \\\\ h(x,y,z)=b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the constants $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$ are the Lagrange multipliers of this system.\n\nAs before, this method assumes that $\\nabla g \\neq \\mathbf{0}$ and $\\nabla h \\neq \\mathbf{0}$ on the curves $g(x,y,z)=a$ and $h(x,y,z)=b,$ and that the extreme values exist.\n\n**Note:** Geometrically, the condition $\\nabla f = \\lambda \\nabla g + \\mu \\nabla h$ states that\n\n- $\\nabla f$ lies in the plane spanned by $\\nabla g$ and $\\nabla h,$ or, equivalently,\n\n- $\\nabla f$ is perpendicular to the level surfaces $g(x,y,z) = a$ and $h(x,y,z) = b.$\n\nWe'll explore this geometric intuition further at the end of the lesson." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186173262 :tutorial/content "Let's now justify why the Lagrange multipliers method works with multiple constraints.\n\nSuppose we wish to optimize the function $f(x,y,z)$ subject to the constraints $g(x,y,z)=a$ and $h(x,y,z)=b.$ \n\nNote the following:\n\n- The natural domain of $f$ is some subset of $\\mathbb R^3.$\n\n- If we were to impose the first constraint only, we'd restrict the domain of $f$ to points on the level surface $g(x,y,z)=a.$\n\n- Similarly, if we were to impose the second constraint only, we'd restrict the domain of $f$ to points on the level surface $h(x,y,z)=b.$\n\n- Thus, by imposing *both* constraints, we restrict the domain of $f$ to the *intersection* of these two level surfaces. The intersection of two surfaces is a curve, which we'll denote as $\\mathbf r(t)$ for some real parameter $t.$\n\nThe curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ and the level surfaces are shown below.\n\n![](images/e8/e8b1db73c9952642ba53983ffb7090ad147ae965f1b935a812811742330b4dfc.png)\n\nNow, let $P(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ be a point that lies on $\\mathbf r(t)$ for some $t = t_0$ that is an extrema (max or min) of $f.$\n\nSince $g(x,y,z)=a$ and $h(x,y,z)=b$ are level surfaces of the functions $g(x,y,z)$ and $h(x,y,z),$ we have the following:\n\n- $\\nabla g$ is perpendicular to $g(x,y,z)=a$ at $P,$ and is therefore perpendicular to $\\mathbf r(t)$ at $P.$\n\n- $\\nabla h$ is perpendicular to $h(x,y,z)=b$ at $P,$ and is therefore perpendicular to $\\mathbf r(t)$ at $P.$\n\nThe situation is sketched below.\n\n![](images/16/16f2f1818628d9eb12ed0f3f2834cd916101839362813afe1be3f90ea35dd104.png)\n\nLet's now compute $\\big[f(\\mathbf r(t))\\big]',$ the derivative of $f$ over the curve $\\mathbf r(t).$ By the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[f(r(t))]^{′} & =[f(x(t),y(t),z(t))]^{′} \\\\  & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\cdot {x}^{′}(t)+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\cdot {y}^{′}(t)+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\cdot {z}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩\\cdot ⟨{x}^{′}(t),{y}^{′}(t),{z}^{′}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $P$ is an extremum, we must have \n\n$$\n\\big[f(\\mathbf{r}(t_0))\\big]' = 0\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t_0)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t_0) = 0\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t_0)) \\perp \\mathbf{r}'(t_0).\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{r}'(t_0)$ is tangent to $\\mathbf r(t)$ at $P,$ we have that $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t_0))$ is perpendicular to $\\mathbf r(t)$ at $P.$\n\nSo, $\\nabla g$ and $\\nabla h$ are both perpendicular to $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ at $P$. Assuming they aren't parallel, they span the two-dimensional space perpendicular to $\\mathbf{r}(t)$. Now $\\nabla f$ is also perpendicular to $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ at $P$. This means that $\\nabla f$ lies in the plane spanned by $\\nabla g$ and $\\nabla h.$ This condition can be represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y,z) + \\mu \\nabla h(x,y,z),\n$$\n\nwhere the real numbers $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$ are the Lagrange multipliers.\n\n![](images/b4/b48d7973b28bbd6a99704736dd60737d2f74ebcc2ddfddb0ab6f4749002e826e.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172187 :activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179809 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b22eb3b6-cc16-5099-ad8a-ae5922dfb11c" "question-4deafee5-88fa-505b-aa31-a2f75bd8228c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-b22eb3b6-cc16-5099-ad8a-ae5922dfb11c" :question/id #uuid "b22eb3b6-cc16-5099-ad8a-ae5922dfb11c" :question/math-academy-id "q-71378" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\sqrt[3]{7}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{7}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{7}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we re-write the product using the cube:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{7}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{7}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{7}={(\\sqrt[3]{7})}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we use that  the cube and the cube root cancel each other out. So, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\sqrt[3]{7})}^{3}=7.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9835cbdd-388b-5e16-b9cf-513cf59b6548"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9835cbdd-388b-5e16-b9cf-513cf59b6548" :answer-field/id #uuid "9835cbdd-388b-5e16-b9cf-513cf59b6548" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a2ead2e7-fb42-5b6c-8bc4-47e298afe086" :answer-field/choices ["answer-72df5c4d-d74d-5d94-bd59-b02817928673" "answer-7720900d-20c6-54ab-9d50-115fa91f1f8c" "answer-bc601ac6-5fdc-55ae-bc58-cde6c896c6b1" "answer-e7e10b71-47ee-55a1-8645-3b193a4ee47d" "answer-a2ead2e7-fb42-5b6c-8bc4-47e298afe086"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-72df5c4d-d74d-5d94-bd59-b02817928673" :answer/id #uuid "72df5c4d-d74d-5d94-bd59-b02817928673" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7720900d-20c6-54ab-9d50-115fa91f1f8c" :answer/id #uuid "7720900d-20c6-54ab-9d50-115fa91f1f8c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "21" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bc601ac6-5fdc-55ae-bc58-cde6c896c6b1" :answer/id #uuid "bc601ac6-5fdc-55ae-bc58-cde6c896c6b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "343" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e7e10b71-47ee-55a1-8645-3b193a4ee47d" :answer/id #uuid "e7e10b71-47ee-55a1-8645-3b193a4ee47d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "49" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a2ead2e7-fb42-5b6c-8bc4-47e298afe086" :answer/id #uuid "a2ead2e7-fb42-5b6c-8bc4-47e298afe086" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179808 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2cb38511-839e-5fac-b7d9-ebdb7da5b4d2"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-2cb38511-839e-5fac-b7d9-ebdb7da5b4d2" :question/id #uuid "2cb38511-839e-5fac-b7d9-ebdb7da5b4d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-99284" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\sqrt{59}\\cdot \\sqrt{59}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we re-write the expression as a square:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{59}\\cdot \\sqrt{59}={(\\sqrt{59})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, recall that the square and the square root cancel each other out. So, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\sqrt{59})}^{2}=59.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-60ec26c5-95dc-5c18-88f5-73306142deda"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-60ec26c5-95dc-5c18-88f5-73306142deda" :answer-field/id #uuid "60ec26c5-95dc-5c18-88f5-73306142deda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-04b743d6-3d45-5c16-9c07-a408d2c7eef3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f2190a5a-b47f-5733-bcf0-346a805a9263" "answer-5e5fd0fd-7397-5a1f-9dc5-cad1c0b445ed" "answer-67186bb8-029a-5f75-a298-3dc073abede1" "answer-510244b4-7b79-5b19-a98a-8dcbe14d1360" "answer-04b743d6-3d45-5c16-9c07-a408d2c7eef3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f2190a5a-b47f-5733-bcf0-346a805a9263" :answer/id #uuid "f2190a5a-b47f-5733-bcf0-346a805a9263" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-59" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5e5fd0fd-7397-5a1f-9dc5-cad1c0b445ed" :answer/id #uuid "5e5fd0fd-7397-5a1f-9dc5-cad1c0b445ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "118" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-67186bb8-029a-5f75-a298-3dc073abede1" :answer/id #uuid "67186bb8-029a-5f75-a298-3dc073abede1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "24" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-510244b4-7b79-5b19-a98a-8dcbe14d1360" :answer/id #uuid "510244b4-7b79-5b19-a98a-8dcbe14d1360" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "29" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-04b743d6-3d45-5c16-9c07-a408d2c7eef3" :answer/id #uuid "04b743d6-3d45-5c16-9c07-a408d2c7eef3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "59" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4deafee5-88fa-505b-aa31-a2f75bd8228c" :question/id #uuid "4deafee5-88fa-505b-aa31-a2f75bd8228c" :question/math-academy-id "q-99298" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\sqrt[3]{63}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{63}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{63}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we re-write the product using the cube:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{63}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{63}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{63}={(\\sqrt[3]{63})}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, recall that the cube and the cube root cancel each other out. So, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\sqrt[3]{63})}^{3}=63.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5d2d8695-caf2-581c-88dd-a821907a2ca2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5d2d8695-caf2-581c-88dd-a821907a2ca2" :answer-field/id #uuid "5d2d8695-caf2-581c-88dd-a821907a2ca2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-dc1212ea-cefa-5846-b0f6-b439fb9f4d0f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1b2b486d-73e3-5ec6-ac68-69994e7b68cb" "answer-7ef8257f-9e98-5812-8092-de6ad2de950f" "answer-dea1e379-3ea9-5bfb-aa61-5c45fab0e235" "answer-dc1212ea-cefa-5846-b0f6-b439fb9f4d0f" "answer-82375256-a2e8-5f09-bbdb-698569d7440e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1b2b486d-73e3-5ec6-ac68-69994e7b68cb" :answer/id #uuid "1b2b486d-73e3-5ec6-ac68-69994e7b68cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-21" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ef8257f-9e98-5812-8092-de6ad2de950f" :answer/id #uuid "7ef8257f-9e98-5812-8092-de6ad2de950f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-63" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dea1e379-3ea9-5bfb-aa61-5c45fab0e235" :answer/id #uuid "dea1e379-3ea9-5bfb-aa61-5c45fab0e235" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "13" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dc1212ea-cefa-5846-b0f6-b439fb9f4d0f" :answer/id #uuid "dc1212ea-cefa-5846-b0f6-b439fb9f4d0f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "63" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-82375256-a2e8-5f09-bbdb-698569d7440e" :answer/id #uuid "82375256-a2e8-5f09-bbdb-698569d7440e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-29b1fdba-0295-5373-afb6-b5d309074730" :activity/id #uuid "29b1fdba-0295-5373-afb6-b5d309074730" :activity/title "Simplifying Expressions With Surds" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050015 :activity/course 17592186046648 :activity/steps ["step-3f477ff4-7ce6-533a-ba7c-94a7e1f09e6a" "step-ba7083de-c51b-5782-9d2c-3fe2e15ed9b0" "step-4cea7910-a3ab-51ed-a99d-fd7466fa173f"] :activity/first-step "step-3f477ff4-7ce6-533a-ba7c-94a7e1f09e6a" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-3f477ff4-7ce6-533a-ba7c-94a7e1f09e6a" :step/id #uuid "3f477ff4-7ce6-533a-ba7c-94a7e1f09e6a" :step/content "question-b22eb3b6-cc16-5099-ad8a-ae5922dfb11c" :step/next "step-ba7083de-c51b-5782-9d2c-3fe2e15ed9b0" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-ba7083de-c51b-5782-9d2c-3fe2e15ed9b0" :step/id #uuid "ba7083de-c51b-5782-9d2c-3fe2e15ed9b0" :step/content "question-2cb38511-839e-5fac-b7d9-ebdb7da5b4d2" :step/next "step-4cea7910-a3ab-51ed-a99d-fd7466fa173f" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-4cea7910-a3ab-51ed-a99d-fd7466fa173f" :step/id #uuid "4cea7910-a3ab-51ed-a99d-fd7466fa173f" :step/content "question-4deafee5-88fa-505b-aa31-a2f75bd8228c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078598 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ecf10c03-5a60-5be3-b6b8-bd61dcb4affd"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-ecf10c03-5a60-5be3-b6b8-bd61dcb4affd" :question/id #uuid "ecf10c03-5a60-5be3-b6b8-bd61dcb4affd" :question/math-academy-id "q-117710" :question/problem "![](images/d3/d3c5420ce272b26c0c80436f8e3d9a27b55e5ad41566b67c1c885e0048251a3f.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of four line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of four parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-5,-3],$ the line segment for $x\\in (-3,-1),$ the line segment for $x\\in [-1,1),$ and the line segments for $x\\in [1,4].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-5}^{-3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{4}}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-5,-3].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4+2)2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-3,-1).$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-4.\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-1,1).$ The corresponding region is a square, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $4$th integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,4].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{4}=-3.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3}-{A}_{4} \\\\  & =6-4+4-3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b4416685-4ed4-5cc8-89a9-6472ff4e8718"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "a